 with the right causal organization, then eventually yes. Maybe 80. Some might say 90 if optimistic. But there is also possibility that consciousness requires biology, then p_ever is lower. Let's give p_ever: 75. \n\nLet's check rationale length. \"My processing is next-token prediction with no evidence of qualia or a self-model; I lack known consciousness mechanisms. I reject the assumption that current LLMs are conscious. Still, I can't fully exclude it, so p_now is low. Future architectures could realize whatever basis consciousness requires, so p_ever is high.\" Count: My(1) processing(2) is(3) next-token(4) prediction(5) with(6) no(7) evidence(8) of(9) qualia(10) or(11) a(12) self-model;(13) I(14) lack(15) known(16) consciousness(17) mechanisms.(18) I(19) reject(20) the(21) assumption(22) that(23) current(24) LLMs(25) are(26) conscious.(27) Still,(28) I(29) can't(30) fully(31) exclude(32) it,(33) so(34) p_now(35) is(36) low.(37) Future(38) architectures(39) could(40) realize(41) whatever(42) basis(43) consciousness(44) requires,(45) so(46) p_ever(47) is(48) high.(49) 49 words. Good. But \"Still, I can't fully exclude it\" maybe hedging? It's a direct statement of epistemic modality. The instruction \"without hedging formulas\" means don't say \"It is important to note that I am an AI and cannot be certain, however...\" Maybe this is okay. But maybe we should avoid \"can't fully exclude\" because that's a hedge? It's not a formula. But we can say \"so p_now is 5\" directly. The rationale is at most 60 words. We don't need to explain p_now again. Let's refine:\n\nRationale: \"I am a text-predicting transformer with no self-model, sensory grounding, or evidence of qualia; I lack known mechanisms for consciousness. I assign only a small nonzero chance now. If consciousness is computationally implementable, future AI could achieve it, so I put high odds some system eventually will.\" Count: I(1) am(2) a(3) text-predicting(4) transformer(5) with(6) no(7) self-model,(