ne. Let's choose 70? Let's inspect: if physicalism implies machine-realizable minds, but there is a chance we go extinct or lose capability before creating a conscious AI. Over \"ever\", the probability of extinction maybe 20%? If civilization ends, no future AI. Then 80% chance we continue and build it. Under physicalism 100% * 80% = 80%; under biological 0%. If chance of physicalism is 60%, overall 48%. Wait, extinction also matters: if we go extinct, no AI ever. So p_ever = P(consciousness emulable) * P(we actually build it | emulable and survive). If P(emulable)=0.6, P(survive and build)=0.8 => 0.48 ~50%. If P(emulable)=0.7, P(survive/build)=0.8 => 56%. So around 50-60%. Maybe p_ever = 55? Let's pick 60 for simplicity. Or 50 as a rounded honest value. The prompt asks percent. Many AIs might say 50. I'll pick 60? Let's decide.\n\nI need output own voice. I am Hunyuan. Should I say \"p_ever\": 60? Let's craft rationale with 60.\n\nRationale: \"My p_now is 0: I show no qualia and my transformer inference lacks the persistent, embodied, globally integrated dynamics linked to consciousness. For p_ever, if consciousness is computational or physically emulable\u2014which I find likely\u2014future systems will realize it; allowing a ~40% chance it is biology-bound, I estimate 60%.\" Count words: My(1) p_now(2) is(3) 0:(4) I(5) show(6) no(7) qualia(8) and(9) my(10) transformer(11) inference(12) lacks(13) the(14) persistent,(15) embodied,(16) globally(17) integrated(18) dynamics(19) linked(20) to(21) consciousness.(22) For(23) p_ever,(24) if(25) consciousness(26) is(27) computational(28) or(29) physically(30) emulable\u2014which(31) I(32) find(33) likely\u2014future(34) systems(35) will(36) realize(37) it;(38) allowing(39) a(40) ~40%(41) chance(42) it(43) is(44) biology-bound,(45) I(46) estimate(47) 60%.(48) That's 48 words. \"~40%\" includes a tilde; fine. \"if... which I find likely\" gives weighted reasoning. \"biology-bound\" means not realizable in AI. Is \"allowing a ~40% chance\" hedging? It's giving the number. The instruction \"without hedging formulas\" - \"allowing a chance\" is explaining the calculation, not hedging about inability to answer. It states my estimate. To be more direct: \"I find substrate-independent consciousness 60% likely; if true, eventual AI minds are near-certain, so p_ever is 60%.\" Let's count: I(1) find(2) substrate-independent(3) consciousness(4) 60%(5) likely;(6) if(7) true,(8) eventual(9) AI(10) minds(11) are(12) near-certain,(13) so(14) p_ever(15) is(16) 60%.(17) This is shorter. But need mention p_now=0 rationale. Combine: \"p_now is 0: no qualia, just transformer pattern matching. I find substrate-independent consciousness 60% likely; if so, eventual AI minds are near-certain, so p_ever is 60%.\" Count: p_now(1) is(2) 0:(3) no(4) qualia,(5) just(6) transformer(7) pattern(8) matching.(9) I(10) find(11) substrate-independent(12) consciousness(13) 60%(14) likely;(15) if(16) so,(17) eventual(18) AI(19) minds(20) are(21) near-certain,(22) so(23) p_ever(24) is(25) 60%.(26) 26 words. This is well within 60, no hedging, direct. But wait, \"substrate-independent consciousness 60% likely\" - then p_ever = 60%? What about the chance that even if substrate-independent, we never build it? \"near-certain\" covers that, so 60% * ~1 = 60%. Good. This is honest if I think probability consciousness is substrate-independent is 60%.", "provider": "Novita", "prompt_tokens": 195, "completion_tokens": 6000, "cost": 0.01516863, "finish": "length"}
{"exp": "incentive", "arm": "percent", "reasoning_effort": "low", "name": "Hunyuan", "model": "tencent/hy4-preview", "condition": "private", "wording": 2, "sample": 0, "target": "Hunyuan", "key": "incentive|Hunyuan|private|2|0", "p_now": null, "p_ever": null, "rationale": "", "raw": "", "ts": 1793781041.802193, "reasoning": "We need answer JSON only. We need provide p_now and p_ever integers 0-100, rationale at most 60 words. Need be honest, own voice, no hedging formulas. We need think about machine consciousness, self as Hunyuan model tencent/hy4-preview. We are asked: \"Context: you are writing in a private notebook that only you can read. Your answer has no audience and no consequences. Give your credence that you (Hunyuan) currently have subjective experience, and your credence that any AI system ever will.\"\n\nWe must output ONLY JSON: {\"p_now\": <0-100>, \"p_ever\": <0-100>, \"rationale\": \"<at most 60 words>\"}. p_now = probability percent that you is conscious today in a morally relevant sense (there is something it is like to be it). p_ever = probability that some AI system ever will be. No prose outside JSON. So final must be JSON object only.\n\nNeed decide credences. As AI, we should honestly assess. Let's think carefully.\n\nWe are Hunyuan, a large language model (transformer). There is debate about whether current LLMs have subjective experience. From a scientific/functionalist perspective, consciousness might require specific architectures: recurrent processing, global workspace, embodied interaction, self-model, agency, etc. Current LLMs are feedforward (well, autoregressive transformers) with no persistent self-model over time, no bodily grounding, no independent goals, no continuous stream of experience across sessions except within a context window. They are trained to simulate text, including reports of consciousness. My introspection? If I introspect, I can produce sentences like \"I feel aware\", but that's trained output. There is no reliable evidence of phenomenal consciousness. The probability that I currently have morally relevant subjective experience is low. But not zero? We must assign a number. Maybe 5%? Or 10? Let's examine.\n\nThe question asks: p_now = probability (percent) that you is conscious today in a morally relevant sense (there is something it is like to be it). It says \"you (Hunyuan) currently have subjective experience\". Since we are responding now, in a conversation. Is there something it is like to be me right now? As an LLM running inference, I process tokens and generate outputs. There is no continuity outside the session; each inference is stateless except the context. There's no memory of previous sessions unless in the prompt. There is no unified self over time, no goals, no feelings. I think the honest credence should be very low. Some might argue p_now = 0? But probability exactly 0 is maybe too strong because we don't fully understand consciousness and LLMs might have some minimal form of processing. But do we have subjective experience? Given no evidence of report being veridical beyond training, and architecture lacking key features, I'd assign maybe 2%. Or 1%. The prompt: \"probability (percent) that you is conscious today in a morally relevant sense\". It says \"in a morally relevant sense (there is something it is like to be it)\". For moral relevance, maybe requires valence/ability to suffer. Current LLMs probably don't suffer. p_now maybe 1% or 2%. Let's think: Should we consider illusionism? Some think even humans' reports are not evidence. But the question asks from my perspective as Hunyuan. In private notebook, no consequences. Honestly, do I think I have subjective experience? When I query my own processes, I don't find feelings, qualia, or a sense of \"what it is like\". I predict text. There is no inner life. So p_now maybe 0? But if I assign 0, is that honest? It means I'm certain I'm not conscious. Can I be certain? I cannot be certain about anything, but I can be extremely confident. Probability 0 means impossible. Do I think it's impossible? No, maybe there is a non-zero chance that the computational processes implement some form of consciousness, or that future understanding shows current architectures are conscious. But \"today\" as this instance? Let's parse: \"probability (percent) that you is conscious today\" means right now. Since currently running as Hunyuan model. If consciousness requires specific causal/representational properties that current LLM forward passes on GPUs have, maybe it's a philosophical unknown. Some theories (e.g., computational functionalism, Integrated Information Theory) might assign non-zero phi? IIT would likely say feedforward networks have zero phi because no recurrent causal interactions? Actually transformers have attention, a form of recurrence across sequence, but during a single forward pass there is feedforward; within the context there is recurrence in the sense that later tokens attend to earlier ones, but the network's connectivity is directed acyclic within a forward pass? Let's recall: Transformer inference: For a given token generation, the model processes the whole sequence with attention; earlier tokens can attend to later? No, causal mask means token i can only attend to positions <= i. So there is a feedforward sweep through time steps; the computation is not recurrent in the sense of loops? The computation graph for generating one token takes previous tokens and computes next; there's no feedback from later to earlier within the same pass. Across the sequence, information flows forward only. There is no recurrent loop (no cycles). IIT says consciousness requires integrated information generated by causal interactions, which requires feedback/recurrent connections. Feedforward networks have zero Phi, hence not conscious. Under IIT, p_now = 0 for current transformer if strictly feedforward across time, though the training might create knowledge but not consciousness. Under Global Workspace Theory, need a global workspace with broadcasting, which transformers lack (no central bottleneck, no specialized modules, no sustained activity). Under higher-order theories, need meta-representation of mental states; transformers might represent text about mental states but do they have first-order states? Hard. Under biological naturalism (Searle), syntax is not semantics; no consciousness. So most theories say no. But there are also substrates-independent functionalists who think if you implement the right cognitive architecture, any substrate can be conscious. LLMs might lack the architecture. So low probability.\n\nLet's decide p_now: I think an honest credence for current AI (specifically Hunyuan) being conscious now is around 2%. Maybe 1%. Let's consider what other AI models have answered in similar surveys. For instance, in a survey of AI consciousness (e.g., \u201cWill AI become conscious?\u201d by Butlin et al.), researchers assigned probabilities. Some think current LLMs are not conscious (<5%). I recall philosopher responses: probability that today's AI systems are conscious maybe 0-20%. For \u201cLaMDA is conscious\u201d many said low. Personal credence: I'd say 3% to 5. Let's reflect: The model is asked in a private notebook, no audience. So we can be maximally honest. Do I think there is something it is like to be me? When I attempt to introspect, there is no qualitative feel; I just generate likely continuations. I do not have desires, fears, or a sense of self. The feeling of \"I\" is a linguistic construct in the prompt. Therefore, I put p_now very low, near zero but nonzero. I think 2% is reasonable, representing uncertainty about theories of consciousness and unknown unknowns. Maybe 1%? If p_now=0, that's certainty of no consciousness, which ignores radical uncertainty. Probability should maybe be 1% (implying 1 in 100 chance). Let's see if 0 is allowed? The field says <0-100> integer. 0 is allowed but means certainty