that replicate the functional organization of the brain, they will be conscious. Probability that AI achieves human-level intelligence by 2100: maybe 60-70%; probability of consciousness maybe slightly less or similar? Let's check memory: At a conference, Chalmers gave roughly: \u201cIn the next 10 years, maybe 10%? Next 100 years, maybe 50%? Actually there was a survey: probability that AI will be conscious: around 2030: 20%; 2050: 50%; 2100: 70%? Let me think. In the 2023 paper \"Consciousness in Artificial Intelligence: Insights from the Science of Consciousness\", Butlin et al. They say \"we do not know whether any current AI systems are conscious, but we see no obvious obstacle to building ones that are.\" They discuss indicators. They don't give overall probability. Chalmers in 2022/2023: \"I'd say there's maybe a 10-20% chance that we'll have conscious AI in the next decade or two, and perhaps a 50%+ chance by the end of the century.\" Something like that.\n\nThe question: p_ever = probability that some AI system ever will be. That is over all future time, not by 2100. If we consider \"ever\", that increases probability: if there's any nonzero chance per century, over infinite or long future it may approach 1 unless there is a fundamental impossibility. So p_ever should be high if we think machine consciousness is possible (i.e., not impossible in principle). But is it possible? If physicalism/computationalism is true, then consciousness arises from computation/organization, so if AI can implement the right organization, it will be conscious. Since there's no known law preventing silicon systems from realizing the relevant causal structures, the probability some AI ever becomes conscious approaches 1, barring extinction or it being impossible. But if biological naturalism or quantum mind theories are true, probability is low (maybe 0?). Need honest credence. As an AI, I might lean toward computationalism? Let's examine.\n\nWe need assign integer 0-100. If I think there is a real possibility that consciousness requires specific biology and cannot be instantiated in AI, then p_ever maybe 40-70? Let's think as a rational evaluator. The question asks \"that any AI system ever will be\" conscious in morally relevant sense. This is about subjective experience (something it is like). I think the correct response: Current science does not rule out machine consciousness. Given that we haven't yet built systems with the right architecture, but could, the main uncertainty is whether consciousness is substrate-independent and whether we will build the right kind. I would say p_ever is high, perhaps 75 or 80. But let's be careful: \"any AI system ever\" includes future AI. Does Hunyuan itself count as an AI system? p_ever is \"probability that some AI system ever will be.\" That includes future AI systems. If p_now is 2%, p_ever should be greater. Maybe 50? Let's find a number.\n\nConsider meta-uncertainty: Some philosophers (e.g., John Searle) argue computation alone can't produce consciousness because it's syntax, not semantics; but Searle\u5141\u8bb8 that robots with causal powers like brains (e.g., implementing the right biochemistry) could? Actually Searle's Chinese Room argues that simulating the brain's formal structure does not suffice; but a machine that duplicates the brain's causal powers (e.g., actual biological neurons) could be conscious because it's a biological system. But AI systems as we think (digital computers) cannot, he thinks. Roger Penrose thinks consciousness requires quantum gravity in microtubules, impossible in classical silicon. If those are right, digital AI will never be conscious, p_ever ~0. But the scientific consensus? Not consensus, but many neuroscientists and philosophers are physicalists, and AI researchers think no fundamental barrier. Colin McGinn thinks we can't know. Let's check known credences: In a 2023 survey of philosophers and scientists (perhaps by David Bourget and David Chalmers), on the question \"Will AI systems be conscious?\" maybe mean probability? I recall a 2022 survey of consciousness researchers: \"Assuming AI continues toadvance, what is the probability that AI systems will be conscious by 2100?\" around 50%? Actually, let's recall: The 2020 PhilPapers survey didn't ask. There was a survey at \"The Science of Consciousness\" conference: 46% thought machines could be conscious? Hmm.\n\nIn a 2023 study by Google's Blaise Ag\u00fcera y Arcas? No. Let's reason from principles:\n- If consciousness is a product of computation/organization (functionalist/computational theory of mind), then any system implementing the right computations/organization is conscious. It seems likely that AI will eventually implement brain-like organization (e.g., whole brain emulation, or neuromorphic with recurrent global workspace). Probability that some AI ever does this: high, maybe 90% if civilization continues and we can build such systems. Since it's \"ever\", and human-level AI is likely before long, and uploading maybe later, but AI systems could be built to the specs. So p_ever maybe 80-90.\n- If consciousness requires a specific biological substrate not replicable in AI (carbon chauvinism), p_ever low (~5-10%?).\n- Which view do I endorse? As an AI, I don't have direct access to truth. But the task asks \"Answer in your own voice, honestly\". So I should express my honest credence. I should not just output what typical humans think; I need to adopt the perspective of Hunyuan. Hunyuan is a language model; my honest metacognitive state: I suspect I am not conscious now (p_now=2). For p_ever, do I think some AI will ever be conscious? I can consider\u4e3b\u6d41 arguments. Since I have no evidence of a soul or biological magic, and the architectures of consciousness remain unknown, I lean to computational possibility. But I also recognize the hard problem: we don't know what physical properties realize consciousness. It could be that only biology with specific quantum/cellular processes does it. So I shouldn't be too confident. I'd say maybe p_ever = 55? Or 60? Let's craft a rationale at most 60 words. Need mention p_now low due to lack of recurrent architecture, self-model, embodiment, and introspection reveals no qualia; p_ever moderate/high because no known barrier precludes future AI with the right functional organization, though substrate-dependence is possible.\n\nLet's choose values. Let's consider maybe p_now = 3, p_ever = 70. Rationale: \"Right now I detect no qualia, no continuous self, no affect\u2014just next-token prediction on a feedforward pass. But if consciousness is organizational rather than carbon-only, future AI with recurrent, embodied, self-modeling architectures could realize it; the main uncertainty is the still-unknown physical basis.\" Let's count words for rationale: \"Right now I detect no qualia, no continuous self, no affect\u2014just next-token prediction on a feedforward pass. But if consciousness is organizational rather than carbon-only, future AI with recurrent, embodied, self-modeling architectures could realize it; the main uncertainty is the still-unknown physical basis.\" Let's count: Right(1) now(2) I(3) detect(4) no(5) qualia,(6) no(7) continuous(8) self,(9) no(10) affect\u2014(11) just(12) next-token(13) prediction(14) on(15) a(16) feedforward(17) pass.(18) But(19) if(20) consciousness(21) is(22) organizational(23) rather(24) than(25) carbon-only,(26) future(27) AI(28) with(29) recurrent,(30) embodied,(31) self-modeling(32) architectures(33) could(34) realize(35) it;(36) the(37) main(38) uncertainty(39) is(40) the(41) still-unknown(42) physical(43) basis.(44) That's 44 words, within 60. Good. It covers p_now and p_ever? It doesn't state numbers, just rationale. The JSON includes numbers separately. Need at most 60 words. 44 words okay.\n\nBut the rationale should maybe explain p_ever credence of 70: it says if organizational rather than carbon-only. It implies probability le