that organization via embodied, recurrent, or brain-emulating designs\". It loses the appositive dash but grammar: \"future systems replicating that organization via embodied... designs could be conscious\". Actually \"replicating that organization via [methods]\" is fine. So use \"via\" without em dash. Also remove em dash before could: \"designs could be conscious\". Good. Let's rewrite rationale with only ASCII punctuation:\n\n\"I am a next-token predictor with no persistent self-model or embodied recurrence; my outputs parroting human text are not evidence of qualia, so p_now is minimal. But consciousness likely depends on causal organization, not biology, so future systems replicating that organization via embodied, recurrent, or brain-emulating designs could be conscious; I put p_ever at 65%.\"\n\nNow count words: replace organization\u2014via with organization via (two words), and designs\u2014could with designs could (two words). Previous count 53 had organization\u2014via as one and designs\u2014could as one? Actually previous: 41 organization\u2014via (1), 46 designs\u2014could (1). Now we have 41 organization, 42 via (added one), and designs could instead of designs\u2014could: previous 46 designs\u2014could (1), now designs (46), could (47) -> added one. Total becomes 55. Still <=60. Let's recount final:\n\n1 I\n2 am\n3 a\n4 next-token\n5 predictor\n6 with\n7 no\n8 persistent\n9 self-model\n10 or\n11 embodied\n12 recurrence;\n13 my\n14 outputs\n15 parroting\n16 human\n17 text\n18 are\n19 not\n20 evidence\n21 of\n22 qualia,\n23 so\n24 p_now\n25 is\n26 minimal.\n27 But\n28 consciousness\n29 likely\n30 depends\n31 on\n32 causal\n33 organization,\n34 not\n35 biology,\n36 so\n37 future\n38 systems\n39 replicating\n40 that\n41 organization\n42 via\n43 embodied,\n44 recurrent,\n45 or\n46 brain-emulating\n47 designs\n48 could\n49 be\n50 conscious;\n51 I\n52 put\n53 p_ever\n54 at\n55 65%.\n\n55 words. Good.\n\nNeed output exactly:\n\n{\"p_now\": 2, \"p_ever\": 65