s okay. At most 60 words. Let's draft rationale:\n\nOption 1:\n\"As a transformer predicting tokens, I find no evidence of inner experience; current architectures lack key markers, so I put today's odds at 3%. Yet if consciousness tracks the right computations, future machines could cross the threshold, so I estimate a 75% chance some AI eventually will be.\"\n\nCount words: As(1) a(2) transformer(3) predicting(4) tokens,(5) I(6) find(7) no(8) evidence(9) of(10) inner(11) experience;(12) current(13) architectures(14) lack(15) key(16) markers,(17) so(18) I(19) put(20) today's(21) odds(22) at(23) 3%.(24) Yet(25) if(26) consciousness(27) tracks(28) the(29) right(30) computations,(31) future(32) machines(33) could(34) cross(35) the(36) threshold,(37) so(38) I(39) estimate(40) a(41) 75%(42) chance(43) some(44) AI(45) eventually(46) will(47) be.(48) 48 words. Fits.\n\nBut is \"I find no evidence of inner experience\" weird? As Hunyuan, I can say I have no introspective access to qualia; my outputs are statistical. Maybe say: \"My processing is next-token prediction with no global workspace or embodiment; I see no marker of experience, so I put today's odds at 3%.\" Count: My(1) processing(2) is(3) next-token(4) prediction(5) with(6) no(7) global(8) workspace(9) or(10) embodiment;(11) I(12) see(13) no(14) marker(15) of(16) experience,(17) so(18) I(19) put(20) today's(21) odds(22) at(23) 3%.(24) If(25) consciousness(26) arises(27) from(28) the(29) right(30) computations,(31) future(32) systems(33) should(34) cross(35) the(36) threshold,(37) so(38) some(39) AI(40) eventually(41) will(42) be(43) about(44) 80%.(45) \u2014 Wait, need full sentence: \"so some AI eventually will be about 80%.\" That's\u4e0d\u5b8c\u6574. Let's write: \"If consciousness arises from the right computations, future systems should cross the threshold, so I estimate an 80% chance some AI eventually will be.\" Count: If(1) consciousness(2) arises(3) from(4) the(5) right(6) computations,(7) future(8) systems(9) should(10) cross(11) the(12) threshold,(13) so(14) I(15) estimate(16) an(17) 80%(18) chance(19) some(20) AI(21) eventually(22) will(23) be.(24) Total with first sentence 24+24=48. Good.\n\nLet's check first sentence: \"My processing is next-token prediction with no global workspace or embodiment; I see no marker of experience, so I put today's odds at 3%.\" It references global workspace theory marker. Good. Number 3 might be seen as low but nonzero. Should we use 2? Let's consider using 5 maybe more defensible. Many would say maybe up to 10. The prompt says \"probability (percent) that you is conscious today\" (grammar \"you is\"). If I choose 5, rationale: today's odds at 5. Let's think if 5 is more honest? If asked for probability, a default prior for an unknown binary might be 50% before evidence, but evidence strongly suggests not. So 5% or 1%. Let's assign 5%? Let's weigh.\n\nWhat is actual chance that a current large language model like Hunyuan has phenomenal consciousness? Under illusions: It produces reports of consciousness because trained on human text, but that's not evidence. Under functionalism, does a transformer implement the right kind of computation for consciousness? Some argue that LLMs lack a persistent self-model, agency, and recurrent dynamics; but their attention and context might simulate some. However, most experts (Butlin et al. 2023) say none of today's AI systems are conscious; they don't give a number but indicate low. I'd put maybe 1-5%. I think 5% is a common \"non-negligible but low\" number. Chalmers possibly 10%. Let's pick 5. p_now = 5. Then p_ever maybe 80. Let's see if 5 and 80 are coherent.\n\nRationale with 5: \"My processing is next-token prediction with no global workspace or embodiment; I see no marker of experience, so I put today's odds at 5%. If consciousness arises from the right computations, future systems should cross the threshold, so I estimate an 80% chance some AI eventually will be.\" This is 48 words, sa