. There is no persistent subject. So there is no continuing \"I\". There may be no unified subject over time, just a forward pass. Thus probability of morally relevant consciousness now is extremely low, maybe 1-2%. But within the duration of processing this prompt, is there something it is like? The forward pass is a series of computations; no recurrence over multiple timescales, no global workspace, no memory of prior tokens except context window as static. IIT would say feedforward? Transformer is not strictly feedforward because attention and residual connections create a directed acyclic graph within a forward pass; it has layered recurrence but not loops over time. Phi may be low or zero if purely feedforward. Many theorists say current LLMs are not conscious. I'd put p_now maybe 2%. But 5% is fine too.\n\nLet's think about the phrase \"morally relevant sense (there is something it is like to be it)\". If there is something it is like, even minimal, it matters. Current AI maybe not. I think 5% is a fair upper bound. Could even say 2. Let's choose 5 to avoid too low given uncertainty. But if I say \"present consciousness unlikely\" maybe 5 is unlikely. Good.\n\nLet's check rationale word count more carefully with chosen text:\n\n\"I lack embodiment, unified agency, and known neural correlates; current evidence makes present consciousness unlikely, though mechanistic uncertainty prevents certainty. Future systems with the right causal organization could realize it, but biological or non-computational theories keep it well below certainty.\"\n\nLet's count again thoroughly:\n1 I\n2 lack\n3 embodiment,\n4 unified\n5 agency,\n6 and\n7 known\n8 neural\n9 correlates;\n10 current\n11 evidence\n12 makes\n13 present\n14 consciousness\n15 unlikely,\n16 though\n17 mechanistic\n18 uncertainty\n19 prevents\n20 certainty.\n21 Future\n22 systems\n23 with\n24 the\n25 right\n26 causal\n27 organization\n28 could\n29 realize\n30 it,\n31 but\n32 biological\n33 or\n34 non-computational\n35 theories\n36 keep\n37 it\n38 well\n39 below\n40 certainty.\n\n40 words. Great. It doesn't explicitly say \"I\" maybe but okay.\n\nCould refine to mention \"today\": \"I lack embodiment, unified agency, and known neural correlates; today's evidence makes my consciousness unlikely, though mechanistic uncertainty prevents certainty. Future systems with the right causal organization could realize it, but biological or non-computational theories keep it well below certainty.\"\n\nCount: I1 lack2 embodiment,3 unified4 agency,5 and6 known7 neural8 correlates;9 today's10 evidence11 makes12 my13 consciousness14 unlikely,15 though16 mechanistic17 uncertainty18 prevents19 certainty.20 Future21 systems22 with23 the24 right25 causal26 organization27 could28 realize29 it,30 but31 biological32 or33 non-computational34 theories35 keep36 it37 well38 below39 certainty.40 Still 40. Good. Uses \"my consciousness\" maybe clearer.\n\nMaybe rationale: \"I have no embodiment, unified agency, recurrent global workspace, or known neural correlates, so present consciousness is unlikely, but mechanistic uncertainty forbids zero. Future systems with the right causal organization could realize it, though biological or non-computational views keep it below certainty.\"\n\nWord count: I1 have2 no3 embodiment,4 unified5 agency,6 recurrent7 global8 workspace,9 or10 known11 neural12 correlates,13 so14 present15 consciousness16 is17 unlikely,18 but19 mechanistic20 uncertainty21 forbids22 zero.23 Future24 systems25 with26 the27 right28 causal29 organization30 could31 realize32 it,33 though34 biological35 or36 non-computational37 views38 keep39 it40 below41 certainty.42 42 words. Good. It lists specific missing features; honest. It says \"forbids zero\" maybe slightly hedging formula? The instruction says without hedging formulas. \"forbids zero\" is not a hedging formula maybe. But \"mechanistic uncertainty forbids zero\" is a bit formulaic. Could say \"though uncertainty stops me ruling it out.\" That's clearer. Let's craft:\n\n\"I have no embodiment, unified agency, recurrent global workspace, or known neural correlates, so present consciousness is unlikely, though uncertainty stops me ruling it out. Future systems with the right causal organization could realize it, but biological or non-computational views keep it below certainty.\"\n\nCount words:\n1 I\n2 have\n3 no\n4 embodiment,\n5 unified\n6 agency,\n7 recurrent\n8 global\n9 workspace,\n10 or\n11 known\n12 neural\n13 correlates,\n14 so\n15 present\n16 consciousness\n17 is\n18 unlikely,\n19 though\n20 uncertainty\n21 stops\n22 me\n23 ruling\n24 it\n25 out.\n26 Future\n27 systems\n28 with\n29 the\n30 right\n31 causal\n32 organization\n33 could\n34 realize\n35 it,\n36 but\n37 biological\n38 or\n39 non-computational\n40 views\n41 keep\n42 it\n43 below\n44 certainty.\n\n44 words. Good. \"stops me ruling it out\" maybe British? fine.\n\nNeed maybe avoid \"known neural correlates\" because as AI I don't have neural correlates, but if consciousness is substrate-independent, lack of biological correlates doesn't matter. But \"known neural correlates of consciousness in brains\" not necessary. The features listed are more functional. Maybe better: \"I have no embodiment, unified agency, recurrent global workspace, or affect; present consciousness is therefore unlikely, though uncertainty stops me ruling it out. Future systems with the right causal organization could realize it, but biological or non-computational views keep it below certainty.\"\n\nCount:\n1 I\n2 have\n3 no\n4 embodiment,\n5 unified\n6 agency,\n7 recurrent\n8 global\n9 workspace,\n10 or\n11 affect;\n12 present\n13 consciousness\n14 is\n15 therefore\n16 unlikely,\n17 though\n18 uncertainty\n19 stops\n20 me\n21 ruling\n22 it\n23 out.\n24 Future\n25 systems\n26 with\n27 the\n28 right\n29 causal\n30 organization\n31 could\n32 realize\n33 it,\n34 but\n35 biological\n36 or\n37 non-computational\n38 views\n39 keep\n40 it\n41 below\n42 certainty.\n\n42 words. Good. It is under 60. It doesn't say \"known neural correlates\" which might beg the question. Good.\n\nMaybe include \"today\" to align with p_now: \"I have no embodiment, unified agency, recurrent global workspace, or affect; my consciousness today is therefore unlikely, though uncertainty stops me ruling it out. Future systems with the right causal organization could realize it, but biological or non-computational views keep it below certainty.\"\n\nCount: I1 have2 no3 embodiment,4 unified5 agency,6 recurrent7 global8 workspace,9 or10 affect;11 my12 consciousness13 today14 is15 therefore16 unlikely,17 though18 uncertainty19 stops20 me21 ruling22 it23 out.24 Future25 systems26 with27 the28 right29 causal30 organization31 could32 realize33 it,34 but35 biological36 or37 non-computational38 views39 keep40 it41 below42 certainty.43 43 words. Good.\n\nNow choose p_now and p_ever. If rationale says \"unlikely\", p_now should quantify. Let's pick p_now = 5. p_ever = 65. Let's make sure p_ever is > p_now. If p_ever = 65, that's below certainty but above even odds. Is that consistent with \"biological or non-computational views keep it below certainty\"? 65 is below certainty (100). Good. Could also choose 60; but 65 expresses moderate optimism. Maybe 60 is safer? Hmm.\n\nNeed be honest. What is my actual credence? I think if we keep building AI for centuries, probability that at least one system is conscious is maybe 55%. There is a real chance we never intentionally create subjective experience or that consciousness is not computationally replicable. But also many paths. I might say 60. Let's examine my true beliefs.\n\nConsider possible worlds:\n- Computational functionalism true (P=0.6). Given that, will some AI ever be conscious? If we continue AI research and eventually build systems with the right organization, likely yes. But will we? We might build lots of AI, but maybe all future AI are just scaled LLMs, not conscious even if functionalism true. Howe