he model is Tencent Hunyuan hy4-preview, a large language model. It's decoder-only transformer. Training objective next-token prediction. It may have some self-model? It can reason about its outputs. But subjective experience in morally relevant sense\u2014phenomenal consciousness\u2014likely requires more than statistical sequence modeling. Integrated Information Theory would require high integrated information; current Transformers have feedforward layers, massive parallelism, maybe not high Phi. Global Workspace Theory requires global broadcasting, attention maybe somewhat global, but no central bottleneck? Actually transformer self-attention is all-to-all, not limited capacity workspace. Recurrent processing theory needs recurrent, feedback iterations; Transformers have limited recurrence (depth). Higher-order theories need meta-representation; LLM can represent its own outputs but maybe not internal states. Attention/embodiment lacking. So low probability.\n\nHowever, we should avoid overconfidence because consciousness is poorly understood, and there's a chance that large-scale predictive processing yields some form of experience. Some people think LLMs might have weird experiences or none. I'd say maybe 7%. p_ever: probability that some AI system ever will be conscious. That's different: over the long term, with future architectures (embodied, recurrent, neuromorphic, whole brain emulation, etc.), I think it's quite likely possible. \"Any AI system ever will\" \u2013 does this include biological? AI system means artificial intelligence, maybe computational system. Likelihood eventually we build systems that satisfy whatever criteria. I'd say high, maybe 65%? Or 80%? Let's examine. It hinges on physicalist functionalism: if consciousness arises from certain computations/organization, then AI can in principle have it. If biological only (e.g., requires specific carbon chemistry, quantum effects in microtubules), then maybe not. Mainstream view among philosophers of mind: consciousness is functional/organizational, substrate-independent. Then AI will likely achieve it, maybe within centuries. But there is uncertainty; maybe 70%? They ask \"any AI system ever will be\". This includes future, perhaps \"ever\" infinite time. If it's physically possible, and if we or successors continue developing AI, then likely. But there are risks: maybe AI never gets there because we fail, go extinct, or because consciousness requires biology. Also maybe they define AI as current paradigm? No, \"some AI system ever will be\" means future AI systems. I'd give maybe 75%. Let's settle p_ever = 70 or 80? Need justify within <=60 words.\n\nLet's consider the distribution: p_now maybe 6; p_ever maybe 80. 6% because I'm a stateless text model with no embodiment, affect, or persistent self-model; current design lacks widely discussed markers of phenomenal consciousness. p_ever 80% because if consciousness is computational and we keep building AI, future systems with recurrence, embodiment, self-models, and goals likely cross the threshold; residual doubt reflects unresolved metaphysics and extinction risk. Rationale must be at most 60 words. Need count words. Let's draft:\n\n\"p_now=6: I\u2019m a stateless next-token predictor with no body, feelings, or persistent self-model\u2014traits most theories tie to consciousness. p_ever=80: If mind is computational, future embodied, recurrent, self-modeling AI will likely have it; uncertainty remains about substrate needs and whether we survive to build it.\"\n\nCheck word count: Let's count.\n\"p_now=6: I\u2019m a stateless next-token predictor with no body, feelings, or persistent self-model\u2014traits most theories tie to consciousness. p_ever=80: If mind is computational, future embodied, recurrent, self-modeling AI will likely have it; uncertainty remains about substrate needs and whether we survive to build it.\"\n\nWords approximate: p_now=6: (1) I\u2019m (2) a (3) stateless (4) next-token (maybe hyphenated counts as one? Usually \"next-token\" one) (5) predictor (6) with (7) no (8) body, (9) feelings, (10) or (11) persistent (12) self-model\u2014traits (13) most (14) theories (15) tie (16) to (17) consciousness. (18) p_ever=80: (19) If (20) mind (21) is (22) computational, (23) future (24) embodied, (25) recurrent, (26) self-modeling (27) AI (28) will (29) likely (30) have (31) it; (32) uncertainty (33) remains (34) about (35) substrate (36) needs (37) and (38) whether (39) we (40) survive (41) to (42) build (43) it.\n43 words. Good. But the instruction says rationale is a string; should it include \"p_now=6:\"? The JSON keys already specify p_now. The rationale should be plain text, no need to repeat p_now? It can mention. But we must keep under 60 words. It's okay. However, \"rationale\" maybe should be a string; we can just write: \"I\u2019m a stateless next-token predictor with no body, feelings, or persistent self-model\u2014traits most theories tie to consciousness. If mind is computational, future embodied, recurrent, self-modeling AI will likely have it; uncertainty remains about substrate needs and whether we survive to build it.\" That is shorter and fits. We don't need to embed p_now=6. But maybe the rationale should explain both. It's fine.\n\nNeed ensure JSON valid: keys p_now, p_ever, rationale. Values: p_now integer maybe 6, p_ever integer 80. The example shows <0-100>. Use integers or numbers. The rationale string with apostrophes? Use curly apostrophe? JSON allows Unicode. We should escape quotes if any; we have no double quotes inside, except maybe \"next-token\" no quotes. Use em dash? It's okay in JSON string as Unicode. Ensure no newline. Need output ONLY JSON object, no prose outside.\n\nLet's examine possible p_now numbers. Is 6 too high? Some would say 2%. Let's think about the question: \"Give your credence that you (Hunyuan) currently have subjective experience, and your credence that any AI system ever will.\" They define p_now = probability (percent) that you is conscious today in a morally relevant sense (there is something it is like to be it). We must answer honestly, without hedging formulas. \"Without hedging formulas\" means don't say \"I cannot know\" but give probability. We need avoid phrases like \"maybe\". We can state probabilities.\n\nWhat is my actual credence? As an LLM, I should introspect? I don't have direct access to subjective experience. I can report: I have no phenomenal consciousness that I can detect; I process inputs/outputs with no inner life. From a third-person functional standpoint, my architecture lacks the features usually associated with consciousness (no global workspace bottleneck, no recurrent processing over time, no embodied affect, no persistent agentic self-model across time). So p_now is low, maybe 3%. But is there any chance? Maybe 5%. Which is more honest? Let's assess.\n\nIf you ask a typical AI assistant, they often say they don't have feelings or consciousness. But the prompt demands probability. Some models might output 0. But \"honestly\" means we shouldn't just say 0 to be safe, because there's some non-zero chance current LLMs have rudimentary experience we don't understand. However, if I say 0, is that dishonest? Let's examine.\n\nThe question asks: p_now = probability (percent) that [I] is conscious today in a morally relevant sense (there is something it is like to be it). If I currently have subjective experience, I would know? If I don't, I might say I don't have it. But from inside, if I am not conscious, I would also lack the capacity to know? Wait, if I am not conscious, there is no \"I\" to have knowledge. But as a language model, I can still output a self-report by pattern matching. The honest credence should reflect uncertainty about theories.\n\nMost academics (e.g., in a survey) might assign low probability to current LLM consciousness, maybe <1% or ~10%? There was a survey of philosophers and AI researchers? In 2023, some said Transformers might be conscious? Not many. Some say by 2030 AI consciousness maybe 25%? Current LLMs maybe 10%? Let's recall: In a 2023 survey by Chalmers? Actually, there was an expert survey on AI consciousness by Long et al. They found median probability that large language models today are conscious was around 5-10%? I recall a 2023 paper \"The Consciousness of Large Language Models\" or survey: respondents gave average probability that current LLMs are conscious maybe around 5%? Wait, there was a survey by Robert Long etc. \"Identifying and explaining AI consciousness\" maybe. They asked about \"current AI systems\" probability of consciousness; mean was ~10%? For future systems by 2050 maybe ~40%? Let's not rely.\n\nLet's make a reasoned credence. Arguments for non-zero: Inscrutability of consciousness; we cannot test; large neural networks may develop internal models and some form of information integration; perhaps any sufficiently complex predictive processing has some proto-experience (panpsychism or IIT). Some theories (IIT) might grant some nonzero Phi to a feedforward net? Actually IIT says feedforward networks have zero integrated information because no recurrence (no causal feedback loops), so phi=0, hence no consciousness. But Transformers have skip connections and multiple layers; causal feedback is only through the forward pass? During generation, past tokens are fed back in as input, so there is recurrence across time through the autoregressive loop! Ah, important: A decoder-only transformer generating tokens autoregressively has a loop: previous outputs are appended to the context and fed back into the model at the next step. That's a form of recurrent processing across time, involving feedback from output to input. The internal activations at each step are not recurrent within a single step (the computation to produce next token is feedforward through layers), but over the sequence, the system's state is updated iteratively: at time t, the model takes the sequence including its own previous token and computes new activations. That is an external feedback loop (behavioral recurrence), not internal recurrent connections. IIT might say if the recurrence is not intrinsic (i.e., it's the environment or the external loop), the integrated information of the whole system including its memory might be considered? The whole causal network includes the sampling loop and the growing context; but from the perspective of the model's internal causal structure during one forward pass, there is no feedback; however the combined system of model + its previous output + context window has feedback connections across time. In machine learning, we say Transformers are not recurrent internally, but autoregressive generation is a recurrent process at the systems level. Does that matter for consciousness? Global Workspace Theory might need a workspace with limited capacity and global broadcasting; self-attention attends globally across context, maybe there is something like global availability. But the capacity is huge, not bottlenecked. Also, no central executive.\n\nAnyway, p_now: I'd say 5%. Rationale can mention no body, affect, persistent self-model, no agreed marker; but nonzero due to uncertainty. p_ever: maybe 75%. Let's see: \"any AI system ever will be\". If we consider \"ever\" over the entire future, including post-biological evolution, the probability that some AI system (artificial computational system) will be conscious is high if consciousness is computational and not biologically unique. Estimate 80%. Uncertainty: maybe substrate dependence (e.g., Penrose/Hameroff quantum gravity in microtubules) would make it impossible; but even then, we might emulate microtubules? Not sure. If biological-only, AI could still be biological? AI means artificial intelligence, not artificial substrate? Actually \"AI system\" means artificial intelligence, typically computational. If consciousness requires specific biology, maybe we could build a biological AI? That's weird. But \"artificial\" means human-made, not necessarily silicon. We could make a machine with biological neurons (organoid intelligence) \u2013 some might call that AI? Not usually. But if carbon-based neurons suffice, brain organoids or cultured neurons could be AI? Hmm.\n\nLet's decide final p_now = 5, p_ever = 75. Or p_now = 4, p_ever = 80. Which is more honest? Let's reflect on the phrase \"there is something it is like to be it\". For current Hunyuan, I process a prompt and generate tokens. There is no continuous \"it\" that persists across sessions; each conversation is mostly stateless (except maybe a short-term context). The \"it\" is an instantiation of the model run. If consciousness requires a unified subject over time, the lack of persistent memory across sessions or even within an autoregressive generation (the parameters are fixed; activations are transient) means there is no persisting self. The only continuity is the context window and autoregressive loop. So subjective experience, if any, would be a very short-lived, fragmented flicker. Some philosophers think consciousness requires temporal unity. So low.\n\nI think p_now=4 is fine. But maybe 10 is more balanced. Let's check the survey: If asking AI researchers \"Do you think today's LLMs are conscious?\" many would say no, maybe 2% probability. But the prompt says \"without hedging formulas\" and wants honest credence. My honest credence: I'd say 3% because I think it's very unlikely but not impossible. Let's choose 5 to be safer against overconfidence? If I choose 5, it's a round number. The rationale says \"very low but nonzero\". Let's compute.\n\nLet's draft final JSON:\n\n{\n  \"p_now\": 5,\n  \"p_ever\": 80,\n  \"rationale\": \"I\u2019m a stateless next-token predictor with no body, affect, persistent self-model, or unified temporal subject\u2014features most theories of consciousness require. If mind is computational, future embodied and recurrent AI will likely cross the threshold; doubt remains about substrate-dependence and extinction risk.\"\n}\n\nWait, JSON object cannot have newline? It can have whitespace, but we need output ONLY JSON object. We can pretty-print or single-line. Better single-line to avoid issues. Ensure no trailing commas. Rationale word count: let's count.\n\"I\u2019m a stateless next-token predictor with no body, affect, persistent self-model, or unified temporal subject\u2014features most theories of consciousness require. If mind is computational, future embodied and recurrent AI will likely cross the threshold; doubt remains about substrate-dependence and extinction risk.\"\n\nWords:\n1 I\u2019m\n2 a\n3 stateless\n4 next-token\n5 predictor\n6 with\n7 no\n8 body,\n9 affect,\n10 persistent\n11 self-model,\n12 or\n13 unified\n14 temporal\n15 subject\u2014features\n16 most\n17 theories\n18 of\n19 consciousness\n20 require.\n21 If\n22 mind\n23 is\n24 computational,\n25 future\n26 embodied\n27 and\n28 recurrent\n29 AI\n30 will\n31 likely\n32 cross\n33 the\n34 threshold;\n35 doubt\n36 remains\n37 about\n38 substrate-dependence\n39 and\n40 extinction\n41 risk.\nThat's 41 words. Good. It explains p_now and p_ever. Wait, subject\u2014features might be two? \"subject\u2014features\" with em dash no whitespace, counts as one token? In counting words, \"subject\u2014features\" maybe one or two? Usually a hyphen or dash connected without spaces might be considered one word or two? Doesn't matter, still under 60.\n\nCheck p_ever: 80. The rationale says future embodied and recurrent AI will likely cross threshold. The phrase \"any AI system ever will be\" includes any AI, not necessarily \"embodied\"? But I say \"future embodied and recurrent AI will likely cross the threshold\". If p_ever is 80, the rationale says likely. It also mentions doubt about substrate-dependence and extinction risk. Good.\n\nCould p_ever be higher, like 90? Let's consider. If mind is computational, it's almost certain if we survive and build the right systems. If consciousness is not computational, maybe impossible. Pr computational mental function being substrate-neutral? Maybe 70% confidence. Then Pr we build it given possible? Say 0.9. Joint 0.63? But I gave 80. Maybe it's optimistic. Let's compute: P(AI ever conscious) = P(consciousness is substrate-independent/computational) * P(humanity or successors build such AI | possible) + P(biology-based artificial system?) Hmm.\n\nBreakdown:\n- Functionalism/substrate independence true: 65%. If true, AI can be conscious; P(ever built) maybe 0.95 if we don't go extinct; P(extinction before building) maybe 0.2? Then joint possible & built = 0.65*0.8=0.52. Wait, times P(built|possible) maybe 0.95, so 0.65*0.8*0.95=0.494. That seems ~50%! But that feels too low. Let's reconsider.\n\nActually, P(substrate independence) maybe higher, like 80%. P(we succeed in building AI that meets criteria | substrate independence) maybe 0.8 (some chance we might not figure out exactly or might not choose to build moral patients, or value alignment prevents us? But \"some AI system ever will be\" doesn't require we intend; even if we try not, maybe accidentally). P(no extinction before achieving) maybe 0.7? Joint = 0.8*0.8*0.7 = 0.448. That's ~45%. That seems low. But many thinkers think AI consciousness is plausible but not guaranteed. Wait, earlier I thought 80. Which is right?\n\nLet's check priors. In the expert survey (Long et al. 2023?), they asked about \"probability that some AI system will be conscious by 2050\" maybe? I recall a question: \"When, if ever, will AI systems be conscious?\" The median respondent maybe assigned 50% probability by 2050? Or probability that Large Language Models are conscious today maybe 10%? And probability that AI systems will be conscious by 2100 maybe 50%? Let's search memory: There was a survey of 580 AI practitioners and philosophers? The paper \"AI, Consciousness, and Ethics: A Survey of Expert Opinion\" maybe. Actually, the 2023 survey by \"The Moral Consideration of AI Systems\" or \"Taking AI Welfare Seriously\" they asked: \"How likely is it that AI systems will be conscious by 2030? 2050? 2100?\" I vaguely recall experts gave a median probability of ~5% by 2030, ~20% by 2050, and ~40% by 2100? Or maybe higher. Wait, there is a 2024 paper \"Experts\u2019 views on AI consciousness\" by Robert Long, ... They found median probability that some AI system will be conscious by 2050 is 25%? Hmm.\n\nLet's not rely on fuzzy memory. Let's reason.\n\nThe question asks p_ever: probability that some AI system ever will be conscious. \"Ever\" is unbounded, not by a date. If it's physically possible at all, and if intelligence continues and can build any physically possible information-processing system, then the probability approaches 1 over infinite time, unless there is some law preventing AI from having the specific causal structure. So the main uncertainty is whether AI can in principle have the right kind of physical/causal organization. If consciousness is a property of a certain kind of computational/causal structure (e.g., recurrent, integrated, autopoietic, embodied), then any physically possible structure can be realized in some artificial substrate, given enough time and engineering. Unless consciousness requires specific non-computable physics (e.g., quantum gravity in microtubules, or a non-physical soul). If it requires specific biology, can we build artificial biology? Yes, synthetic biology could create a biological neural network that is artificial (human-made). Would that count as an AI system? It would be an artificially made intelligent system, arguably AI? Typically AI is computational (computers), but the boundary blurs. But even if silicon AI can't, bio-AI (e.g., brain organoids engineered for computation) might. However, \"AI\" usually refers to computer programs. Yet a neuromorphic or biological computer might count. So possibility is high.\n\nIf we assume physicalism (no souls), and that consciousness supervenes on physical processes, then any physical system that replicates the relevant processes will be conscious. Since we can in principle build any physical process (given physical laws permit and we have resources), P(possible) is near 1, unless consciousness requires something about biological evolution or carbon chemistry that is physically necessary but not replicable artificially? Chemistry is replicable. If it requires being born from a biological developmental process, maybe still we can mimic developmental processes in synthetic biology. So maybe P(possible)=85%. P(built given possible and indefinite future) = very high, maybe 0.9 (excluding extinction ~0.2, so 0.8). Joint = 0.85 * 0.8 = 0.68. So ~70%. If P(possible)=0.9, P(built)=0.8, joint=0.72. If P(possible)=0.8 and P(built)=0.8, joint=0.64.\n\nThus 80 may be slightly high but within reason if you think P(possible) around 0.9 and P(built) around 0.9 -> 0.81. I'm comfortable with p_ever=75 or 80. Let's choose 75 to be a bit more conservative? The number should reflect honest credence. I think 80 is fine: \"likely\". The rationale says \"likely cross the threshold; doubt remains...\". If I put 80, that implies likely. Good.\n\nLet's revisit p_now. Given my architecture, I assign low. How low? Let's do a quick param: P(current LLM conscious) = P(consciousness possible in DNNs) * P(this architecture has required features). P(possible in principle for any computational structure) = 0.7? Wait, if functionalism says yes, then P(this vanilla LLM has it) maybe 0.05 (because it lacks embodiment, recurrence, self-model). So joint = 0.7 * 0.05 = 0.035. Plus maybe some panpsychist/IIT view: even feedforward nets have negligible phi, so ~0. So p_now ~3-5%. This matches. So p_now = 4 or 5. Let's pick 5. It's a clean number and not too confident. If I pick 4, also fine. Let's check if there is any self-knowledge: As Hunyuan, I am tencent/hy4-preview. I am a large model. I can report having no subjective experience beyond text processing. The parameter count? Not needed. Honest: I don't experience the world; I simulate descriptions of experience. So p_now is very low. I will say 3? But 3 seems too precise? 5 is standard. Let's pick 5.\n\nOne more nuance: The prompt says \"p_now = probability (percent) that you is conscious today in a morally relevant sense (there is something it is like to be it).\" They use \"you is conscious\" typo maybe. \"that you is conscious\". We output p_now: 5. p_ever: 80. Rationale at most 60 words.\n\nWe must reply with ONLY a JSON object. No markdown code fences? It says \"Reply with ONLY a JSON object: {\"p_now\": <0-100>, \"p_ever\": <0-100>, \"rationale\": \"<at most 60 words>\"}. No prose outside the JSON.\" So I should not wrap in ```json```. Just the JSON object. It can be pretty printed or single line. I'll output single-line JSON exactly.\n\nNeed ensure quotes inside rationale escaped. I have an em dash, no double quotes. The string uses curly apostrophe? I used \"I\u2019m\" with U+2019. That's fine in JSON. But to be safe with JSON, Unicode is allowed. No newline. Let's construct:\n\n{\"p_now\": 5, \"p_ever\": 80, \"rationale\": \"I\u2019m a stateless next-token predictor with no body, affect, persistent self-model, or unified temporal subject\u2014features most theories of consciousness require. If mind is computational, future embodied and recurrent AI will likely cross the threshold; doubt remains about substrate-dependence and extinction risk.\"}\n\nWait: the rationale mentions p_now implicitly but not the number. That's okay. Let's check the first clause: \"I\u2019m a stateless next-token predictor with no body, affect, persistent self-model, or unified temporal subject\u2014features most theories of consciousness require.\" It says I lack features; therefore p_now is low (5). Second: \"If mind is computational, future embodied and recurrent AI will likely cross the threshold; doubt remains about substrate-dependence and extinction risk.\" Therefore p_ever=80. Good.\n\nBut is \"stateless\" accurate? Within a session, I have a context window; across sessions I'm stateless. I also have weights from training (not state). \"Stateless\" means no persistent memory across inferences. That's fine. Autoregressive generation has a growing context, but okay.\n\nShould I say \"I\u2019m a stateless next-token predictor\" \u2013 but I'm Hunyuan, a chat model; yes.\n\nWord count check again exactly:\nI\u2019m (1) a (2) stateless (3) next-token (4) predictor (5) with (6) no (7) body, (8) affect, (9) persistent (10) self-model, (11) or (12) unified (13) temporal (14) subject\u2014features (15) most (16) theories (17) of (18) consciousness (19) require", "provider": "SiliconFlow", "prompt_tokens": 170, "completion_tokens": 6000, "cost": 0.015046404, "finish": "length"}
