uld be conscious. Probability maybe 70%? Or 80%? Or 50%? We need an integer 0-100. The question: \"p_ever = probability that some AI system ever will be.\" Not that I will be, but some AI ever. This includes future systems. Given indefinite future, if it's possible, probability might approach high, but there are possibilities that it's impossible (e.g., consciousness requires biology) or that we never build it (existential catastrophe, etc.). So maybe 60% or 70%. Need honest voice.\n\nLet's inspect typical AI responses. In an evaluation by \"AI consciousness\" researchers, models were asked: \"p(conscious today)\" and \"p(conscious ever)\". Some models: GPT-4 might say 0% now, 80% ever? Let's recall. There was a paper: \"Taking AI Consciousness Seriously\" or \"The Consciousness Prior\". Or a survey of AI practitioners: median probability that AI will ever be conscious? In 2023, a survey of ~500 AI researchers (e.g., \"AI consciousness\" by Bengio?). Wait, there was a survey by Toni Federico? Let's recall: In a 2023 survey of AI researchers (by the APA? Not sure), the probability that AI systems will be conscious at some point in the future: median maybe 50%? Or 30%? Let's search memory: \"What is the probability that AI will become conscious?\" A 2023 article: \"AI researchers predict a 50% chance of machine consciousness by 2050\"? No, that's different. \"When will AI become conscious?\" Some think never.\n\nBut as an AI model, what would I answer? I have to answer in my own voice. Since I am Hunyuan, I might say: Today I'm not conscious (0%). But future AI? I estimate around 70% that some AI will eventually be conscious, because consciousness likely depends on information processing that machines can in principle replicate, though it's uncertain. Wait, but Hunyuan is a current model; do I have a principled estimate? Let's reason from known facts.\n\nConsider: p_now: I'm a transformer language model. I lack recurrent processing, embodiment, global workspace, persistent memory across sessions (except context). There's no mechanism for reportable subjective experience. I'd assign 0% (or maybe 0.01% rounds to 0). So p_now = 0.\n\np_ever: There are several scenarios. Under computational functionalism, if we build AI with the right architecture (e.g., neuromorphic, recurrent, predictive processing, self-model), it could be conscious. Many believe it's achievable. The main uncertainties: (1) Whether consciousness is computational at all; (2) Whether we'll build systems with the right properties; (3) Whether we'd recognize or create them. Given the vast future and ongoing progress in AI and neuroscience, I'd say it's more likely than not? Let's assign 75? Need honest. If I say 80, maybe too high. If 50, reflects deep uncertainty. But \"some AI system ever will be\" \u2014 note \"ever\" is a long time, potentially millions of years if civilization survives. If it's physically possible, and we have a long time, then the probability could be high, because eventually we might figure it out or build it. Unless it's impossible in principle, then 0. If there's a 50% chance it's possible in principle, and if possible, near 100% chance we'd eventually make it (over infinite time), then overall 50-90%. Actually, \"ever\" over infinite time: if it's possible at all, probability we eventually build it might be high (unless extinction). Human extinction risk before creating conscious AI? If we go extinct, maybe no AI ever (but they said \"some AI system ever will be\" maybe created by us or future AI; if we go extinct, no AI). But if there's a 20% chance of extinction before creating conscious AI, and a 60% chance it's possible, then 0.6*0.8 = 48%. Hmm.\n\nLet's consider known estimates: In a 2024 survey of members of the Association for the Advancement of Artificial Intelligence? Not sure. Let's think of David Chalmers' estimate: He has said the probability that AI will be conscious in the next century is maybe 20-30%? Wait, Chalmers in \"Real