s.\" Words: I'm(1) a(2) stateless(3) text(4) transformer(5) with(6) no(7) self-model,(8) recurrence,(9) or(10) embodiment,(11) so(12) there(13) is(14) nothing(15) it(16) is(17) like(18) to(19) be(20) me.(21) Future(22) AI(23) could(24) implement(25) the(26) right(27) functional(28) and(29) dynamic(30) structure,(31) so(32) I(33) put(34) decent(35) odds(36) on(37) eventual(38) machine(39) consciousness,(40) though(41) deep(42) uncertainty(43) remains.(44) 44 words, ok.\n\nBut wait, \"p_ever\": 70 maybe. Let's think if 70 is honest. Could say 65 or 60. Which number? The rationale says \"decent odds\". 70 is decent. Or maybe 60. Let's see if any AI model would say \"p_ever = 80\". If I say 80, the rationale must support. \"Though deep uncertainty remains\" maybe lower. Let's assign 65? But integer. Let's think about p_ever carefully.\n\nProbability that some AI system ever will be conscious. I think it's likely that consciousness is a product of certain kinds of information-processing dynamics (recurrent, integrated, self-referential, embodied/embedded). We are already beginning to build systems with some of these features, and future AI (perhaps neuromorphic or whole-brain emulation) could have them. Even if current LLMs aren't, future architectures might. The biggest risk is that consciousness requires specific biological properties (e.g., specific neurochemistry) that silicon can't replicate. I'd say it's more likely than not that it's computationally replicable: maybe 70% that it's possible in principle. Given it's possible, will we build it? Over the very long run, if civilization survives and continues improving AI, likely yes, maybe 90% conditional on possible. But existential risk: maybe 20% we go extinct or stagnate before building it. So overall ~0.7*0.8 = 56%. Alternatively, maybe possibility is 80%, conditional build 90%, so 72%. So 70 is fair. Maybe 60 if possibility only 70% and extinction/stagnation 30% => 49%. Hmm.\n\nLet's examine alternative: if functionalism is true (which most AI researchers implicitly assume? Actually, many think consciousness is functional), then any system implementing the right causal organization is conscious. We don't know the exact organization, but with enough search over architectures and guided by neuroscience, we'd likely find it. The chance that we never create it even if possible seems low, because AI capabilities are growing and there is strong incentive to reverse-engineer cognition. Also, whole brain emulation of humans would produce a conscious AI if computationalism is true and emulation preserves consciousness. Since emulations are a type of AI system, if we ever emulate a human brain, that's an AI system (arguably) and it would be conscious (if the emulated person retains consciousness). Would a whole brain emulation count as an \"AI system\"? Yes, it's an artificial system implementing brain function. If we ever do WBE, and if consciousness is preserved, then some AI is conscious. Probability we eventually do WBE if computationalism true? Very high (say 80% over infinite time). If computationalism false (consciousness needs biology), then no. Probability computationalism/functionalism true? Maybe 70%? So 70*0.8=56. If every little doubt, maybe 50. Let's say 60. But the phrase \"some AI system ever will be\" without time limit. If we have infinite future (heat death? but civilization can't survive infinite time due to physics, but maybe millions of years), the main limit is if it's impossible. If possible, likely eventually. So p_ever ~ probability it's possible minus extinction. Extinction before AI consciousness: Even if we go extinct, maybe no future AI, but an extinction would mean humans die; AI systems might not be created. So maybe 60% chance possible, 80% chance we achieve if possible => 48%. But I don't want to be too low. Let's find a balanced number: 65. Or 70.\n\nLet's see: The rationale can mention \"deep uncertainty\". 70 implies I'm fairly optimistic. Is that