r barriers, but some chance remains. Let's do more systematic:\nPossible worlds:\n1. Consciousness requires biology (e.g., biological naturalism): probability maybe 25%. Then AI never conscious (unless we count neuromorphic biocomputers? But \"AI\" as artificial, non-biological; let's say p_ever | biology = 0). Contribution 0.\n2. Illusionism: no phenomenal consciousness exists: probability 15% (then p_ever=0 because \"something it is like\" never exists? Wait if illusionism true, humans aren't conscious. Then \"some AI system will ever be conscious\" in the defined sense is false, because no system has something it is like. So p_ever=0 under illusionism. Right. So illusionism also drives to 0 for the defined phenomenal sense). Contribution 0 * 15% = 0.\n3. Functionalism / computational sufficient: probability maybe 60%. Then if we build AI with right causal powers and continue progress, probability eventually some AI is conscious = high, say 80% (maybe we might not build right architecture, or use only LLM scaling). Contribution 0.6*0.8=0.48.\n4. Other theories (e.g., IIT, AST, etc.) maybe consciousness requires specific dynamics; AI might or might not. But this overlaps. Let's simplify: overall maybe p_ever around 50%. Wait earlier I considered 70. Let's think of expert surveys. In a 2023 survey of consciousness researchers, 39% thought \"AI systems could be conscious in the future\" maybe? Actually I recall a survey: When asked \"Can an AI system be conscious?\" 30% said yes, 40% no, 30% maybe? Hmm. Let's search memory: There is a 2020 survey of philosophers on consciousness (Bourget & Chalmers). They asked about \"Digital computers\" consciousness: 35% said yes, 30% no, 35% other. For \"future AI systems\", maybe similar.Another data point: In a 2024 paper \"Taking AI Welfare Seriously\" (Sebastian et al.), they argue there is a real possibility. They say current systems have low probability, but future systems maybe. They don't give numbers.\n\nThe prompt wants