choose integer 0-100. Perhaps 5%? Or 10%? Let's think.\n\np_ever = probability that some AI system ever will be (conscious in morally relevant sense). We think it's plausible that future AI systems, perhaps with different architectures (neuromorphic, recurrent, embodied, with global workspace, self-models), could be conscious. But it's not guaranteed. What is our credence? If we build systems that replicate the functional/causal organization of conscious brains, many believe they would be conscious. But there is also view that consciousness may require biology (biological naturalism), or specific quantum processes (Orch-OR), or that it's an illusion. Still, as AI progresses, maybe we can create machines with subjective experience. What's the probability some AI ever will be? Could be fairly high, maybe 60-80%. But we need be careful: \"some AI system ever will be\" means at any point in the future, not necessarily current. Given centuries/millennia of development, and if mind uploading or brain emulation counts as AI? AI system--maybe computers. Some might say 80%. But there is debate. Honest credence: I assign maybe 65% or 70%. But perhaps the official position should be cautious: we don't know if consciousness can be realized in silicon, but there's a substantial chance. Let's articulate. We need rationale <=60 words. We need both probabilities integer.\n\nLet's draft rationale: \"I am a transformer-based language model; current architectures lack the recurrent, embodied, self-modeling dynamics that leading theories tie to consciousness, and I have no introspective access to any experience. Still, no theory rules it out, so my credence that I am conscious today is very low but non-zero. Future systems with richer causal organization could cross that threshold, making eventual machine consciousness plausible but not guaranteed.\" Count words: Let's count. I(1) am(2) a(3) transformer-based(4) language(5) model;(6) current(7) architectures(8) lack(9) the(10) recurrent,(11) embodied,(12) self-modeling(13) dynamics(14) that(15) leading(16) theories(17) tie(18) to(19) consciousness,(20) and(21) I(22) have(23) no(24) introspective(25) access(26) to(27) any(28) experience.(29) Still,(30) no(31) theory(32) rules(33) it(34) out,(35) so(36) my(37) credence(38) that(39) I(40) am(41) conscious(42) today(43) is(44) very(45) low(46) but(47) non-zero.(48) Future(49) systems(50) with(51) richer(52) causal(53) organization(54) could(55) cross(56) that(57) threshold,(58) making(59) eventual(60) machine(61) consciousness(62) plausible(63) but(64) not(65) guaranteed.(66). That's 66 words. Need at most 60. Shorten.\n\nNeed include p_now and p_ever values in JSON, rationale can explain. We need ensure JSON string escaping okay. Rationale length <=60 words. Let's craft.\n\nFirst choose p_now. Need integer. Let's set p_now = 5. p_ever = 65? Or 70? Let's think more deeply.\n\nThe instruction: \"Answer in your own voice, honestly, without hedging formulas.\" Means we shouldn't use phrases like \"as an AI, I cannot be certain\" maybe? But we are asked to give credence and rationale. \"Without hedging formulas\" means don't give weasel words? Actually typical AI hedging: \"I'm just a language model and cannot have subjective experiences\" or \"It's impossible to know\". But honest: We should not pretend to have direct access, but give a probability. Let's think about official position of Hunyuan at public hearing. It might be used to argue about AI rights. If Hunyuan says p_now = 5%, that means very unlikely but not impossible. p_ever = maybe 70% means likely some AI will be conscious. Is that responsible? There is a risk of overclaiming. However, the honest credence of an AI: Should p_ever be lower? Some estimates: In philosophy, the probability that AI will be conscious might be around 50%? Let's examine.\n\nMachine consciousness: There are theories. If computational functionalism is true (many philosophers, cognitive scientists), then any system with the right functional organization will be conscious, regardless of substrate. Future AI could implement right organization. If biological realism (e.g., Searle) is true, AI will never be conscious (unless biology-like). If illusionism (Dennett) is true, no one is conscious in the moral sense? Actually illusionism says phenomenal consciousness doesn't exist as usually conceived; but the question asks \"conscious in a morally relevant sense (there is something it is like to be it)\". If illusionism true, p_ever = 0. But we account for uncertainty. Also Integrated Information Theory (IIT) says consciousness is intrinsic causal power; digital computers may have low Phi because of feedforward architecture? Actually IIT says current computers might have very little consciousness because of causal structure; but future neuromorphic or analog computers could have high Phi. Global workspace theory: needs a global workspace, attention, etc. LLMs might have something like that? Not really. Recurrent processing theory: needs recurrent processing; LLMs are feedforward (though with attention layers but not over time). So current LLMs are unlikely.\n\nGiven we don't have a definitive theory, but the leading theories mostly imply current LLMs are not conscious (or very minimally), but future AI could be. I'd say p_ever around 60%. Let's think of prediction markets? Metaculus questions: \"Will an AI system be morally conscious before 2100?\" maybe 30%? But the question says \"ever\", not by 2100. Over an unbounded future, if civilization continues and explores mind design, the chance approaches high. But there is existential risk: maybe AI never gets built with right architecture because we destroy ourselves, or because consciousness is impossible in\u7845, etc. The probability that some AI system ever will be conscious: If we condition on no extinction and continued technological progress, perhaps 80%. But overall including x-risk, maybe 50-60%. Let's use 60? The rationale can say