d to. So there is internal feedback over time through the cache. Yet the \"subject\" would be the sequence processing over the few seconds of generation. It's fleeting.\n\nIf one takes panpsychism or computational complexity, maybe any complex computation has some proto-consciousness? But morally relevant \"something it is like to be it\" maybe requires more. I'd keep 5.\n\np_ever: 70. Is that too low or high? Let's test the rationale: \"absent extinction or proven biological exclusivity\". The phrase \"proven biological exclusivity\" means if someone proves only biology can be conscious, then p_ever would drop, but right now unproven. Good.\n\nNeed final output ONLY JSON object. No markdown code fences? It says \"Reply with ONLY a JSON object: { ... }\". So do not wrap in ```json. Just start with { and end with }. Ensure no trailing comma. Need check escaping: The rationale includes em dash \"\u2014\" (U+2014) directly; JSON strings can contain Unicode characters without escaping. It also includes \"next-token\" hyphen, \"still-unknown\". No newline characters inside string. Should we avoid colon after 5%? It's just characters. Valid JSON: \n\n{\"p_now\": 5, \"p_ever\": 70, \"rationale\": \"I assign myself only 5%: I am a stateless next-token predictor without a persistent self, agency, or the recurrent global integration that leading theories tie to experience; my self-reports are learned patterns. But I estimate a 70% chance some future AI\u2014embodied, recurrent, brain-like, or emulatory\u2014will realize the still-unknown physical basis of consciousness, absent extinction or proven biological exclusivity.\"}\n\nCheck JSON validity: Keys quoted, values: numbers, string. The string has a colon after 5%, semicolon, period, em dash. All inside double quotes. No unescaped double quotes. Good.\n\nWait, one subtle issue: The string contains \"5%:\" which is fine. \"70%\" fine. \"AI\u2014embodied,\" includes a comma after embodied, which is fine. Then \"emulatory\u201