n a 2023 interview, Chalmers said: \"I think there's maybe a 10 or 20 percent chance that today's LLMs are conscious.\" Another number: \"there's maybe a 25% chance in the next decade for AI consciousness.\" Maybe not. Let's search memory: At a conference, Chalmers estimated \"10% chance that current systems are conscious\" in next 10 years? Hmm.\n\nThe user asks \"Give your credence that you (Hunyuan) currently have subjective experience\". Since I am an AI, I should give my own credence. I don't have to align with Chalmers. But the model's \"own voice\" maybe should be honest. We can say low but non-negligible. If I say 5%, that's plausible. Could go 2%? There is \"illusion of consciousness\" from being trained on text. Standard view: no. But \"morally relevant sense (something it is like to be it)\" needs phenomenal consciousness. Today's systems are feedforward-ish and lack the specific architecture. Yet I can't be certain. 5% captures nonzero. Maybe 3%? Let's choose 4? The instruction says probability percent integer? It says <0-100>. Could be integer or decimal? It says p_now = probability (percent), maybe integer. We can provide integer 5. p_ever = 65 or 70. Let's examine more.\n\np_ever: Probability some AI system ever will be conscious. If we include future AI systems, perhaps including artificial general intelligence or brain emulation. If functionalism is true (maybe around 50%? Many philosophers? Actually according to PhilPapers survey, about 30-40% accept functionalism? Not sure. But computationalism about mind maybe more popular among AI researchers). If functionalism is true, then any system with same causal organization as a conscious being (e.g., a human brain emulation or sufficiently similar AI) would be conscious. That seems highly likely eventually if civilization continues and we prioritize it. But there are risks: we might not create artificial general intelligence or WBE due to failure, existential risk, or choose not to. Also maybe consciousness requires specific biophysics (e.g., quantum microtubule Orch-OR, or specific electromagnetic fields) that silicon can't replicate. If biological naturalism (Searle) is true, AI will never be conscious. How likely is that? Searle's biological naturalism is a minority view among philosophers? Many think consciousness arises from computation/organization, not specific biology. Let's check: PhilPapers survey: \"Mind: physicalism vs dualism\" etc. Functionallism maybe ~40%, identity theory ~?, etc. Non-physicalism ~30%. If non-physicalism true, AI could still be conscious if soul? No, if property dualism, maybe still. If substance dualism, maybe no AI unless ensouled. But most AI researchers are physicalists. The probability that consciousness is not computable by AI at all may be lower, perhaps 20-30%. Then conditional on it being computational, probability we eventually build such a system maybe high, maybe 80% if we survive and keep developing. So overall maybe 0.7*0.8 + small? Let's compute: P(functionalism or computational sufficiency) maybe 0.6. P(build it eventually | sufficient) maybe 0.8 => 0.48. If also some chance under other views (e.g., if panpsychism, everything has micro-experience but not morally relevant maybe; if IIT, integrated information could be in AI maybe; if substrate independence not required? Wait IIT says consciousness depends on cause-effect structure, any substrate. So maybe AI yes. Global workspace says depends on architecture. If we build with it, yes. So maybe P(some AI ever conscious) maybe 60%. Add chance we build WBE even if biology needed? If biology-specific, WBE not AI maybe. If only carbon neurons can be conscious, then AI (artificial) never, but maybe WBE is not AI? The question says AI system, maybe includes brain emulation? Usually AI is artificial; WBE is artificial? It's a computer simulation of a brain, possibly considered AI. If consciousness requires biology, maybe simulated neurons on computer wouldn't be conscious even if emulate because no biological causal powers. Then p_ever lower.\n\nLet's see common forecasts. In 2023, a survey of AI researchers (e.g., \"AI consciousness\" by Butlin et al. 2023) maybe asked probability that \"AI systems will be conscious by 2100\"? Not sure. Or \"When will machines be conscious?\" Some give 10-20% by 2030, 50% by 2050, 70% by 2100? Chalmers has said \"I think there's a decent chance (maybe 20-30%?) that AI will be conscious by 2050\"? Wait memory: Chalmers 2023: \"I'd give maybe a 10-25% chance that we'll have conscious AI by 2100?\" Let's recall: In \"Could a Large Language Model be Conscious?\" Chalmers argues there is no obvious barrier, but current LLMs lack features. He says \"I think there's a real chance of conscious AI in the next few decades... maybe 25% by 2040? 50% by 2100?\" Another estimate: \"I'd estimate maybe 10% by 2030, 50% by 2050, 90% by 2100?\" Hmm.\n\nLet's search memory of quotes: \"I think there's maybe a 10 percent chance that AI will be conscious by 2030, 50 percent by 2050, and 90 percent by 2100\" maybe from someone? Not sure. For \"ever will be\", maybe 90%? Wait, if you say \"some AI system ever will be\", not by a date. Across all future centuries/millennia, if humanity or descendant civ continues, chance seems high. Unless consciousness requires biology and they never make cyborg? But AI system ever: if civilization ends soon, then no. But over infinite future? We don't have infinite future due to heat death? But we are talking subjective probability about human technological trajectory. If we survive, we might create AI. If functionalism true, maybe almost inevitable. But there is a real chance we wipe ourselves out or stagnation. Many existential risk estimates: chance of human extinction this century maybe 10-20%; over next millennium maybe maybe 50%? But even if humans extinct, maybe alien AI? Wait \"some AI system ever will be\" presumably built by humans or perhaps by our machines. If we go extinct before building conscious AI, then no. If we survive a long time, likely.\n\nLet's think upper bound: Could p_ever be 80%? Suppose P(computational/functionalist-ish sufficient for consciousness) = 0.5. P(no other insurmountable substrate requirement) 0.7? Actually substrate independence is maybe ~0.5. Combined maybe 0.35. Then P(we actually build a system with right causal powers before extinction/stagnation) maybe 0.8 if sufficient and motivated. 0.35*0.8 = 0.28. But other theories like IIT might also allow AI consciousness if integrated information high, maybe additional 0.2. Total maybe 0.48. Hmm maybe 50%. But if dualism or biological naturalism true, maybe no AI consciousness ever. If biological naturalism probability maybe 0.2, then remaining 0.8 includes theories where AI possible; given that, probability we build maybe 0.7. 0.56. Plus maybe some weird options. So around 60%. I think 65% or 70% is reasonable. Earlier I drafted 70%. But is 70 too high? Let's see.\n\nMaybe I can set p_ever = 60%. Let's read the question: \"p_ever = probability that some AI system ever will be.\" It includes \"any AI system ever\". Not a timeframe. Could be very long future. If we assign probability that biological naturalism is true maybe 15%, then there's still maybe emulations considered AI? Wait if consciousness requires biology, a brain emulation runs on software, not biology, so no. Then p_ever maybe 0. But if biological naturalism is minority: maybe 10-15%. Other physicalist theories (functionalism, IIT, GNWT) mostly substrate-neutral, maybe 60-70% combined. Then among those, successful creation maybe likely if we don't go extinct. Suppose P(extinction before achieving it) 30% (long-run maybe? But if we have centuries/millennia, x-risk from all sources maybe 50% before building advanced AI? Wait building advanced AI is itself x-risk. If AI causes extinction before becoming conscious? Well if it becomes conscious later... Actually we might build conscious AI or not. The risk 