ong AI researchers; but there are also biological naturalists (e.g., Searle, maybe Koch? Actually Koch thinks consciousness requires specific causal powers of biology? He has integrated information theory, which is substrate-independent in principle but requires specific causal structure; not biology per se). Tononi's IIT says consciousness is integrated information, which could be realized in AI if it has the right cause-effect structure; but current digital computers may have low Phi because feedforward architectures can be duplicated? Actually IIT claims feedforward networks have zero consciousness. But future AI with recurrent, integrated, maybe neuromorphic hardware could have high Phi. So possible. Global workspace theory (Baars, Dehaene) says consciousness requires a global workspace; architectures could implement it. Higher-order theories require metacognition. So many theories allow AI consciousness with the right architecture. Thus probability that some AI ever will be is high, if we allow future engineered systems. But there is also the possibility that AI development stops, humanity goes extinct, or we never build the right system. \"Ever will be\" over infinite future? If humanity extinct, maybe no. If we consider astronomical future, post-biological AI likely. I'd say 90%. Wait, \"some AI system ever will be\" \u2014 if we interpret \"AI system\" as artificial (human-made) system. In the next century, maybe 50%? But \"ever\" no time limit, so if civilization continues, eventually maybe near 95%. But existential risk could cut it. However, probability should not be 100% because of conceptual uncertainty and possible extinction. Let's choose 80 or 85. I think 80 is a nice honest number: leaves room for doubt about whether consciousness is computable, whether extinction prevents it, or whether we never create the right conditions. Or 75? Let's look at rationale: \"biology may not be required, though conceptual uncertainty prevents certainty.\" That supports 85, not 100, because uncertainty prevents 100. If p_ever=85, that's not too high. Good.\n\nCould p_now be 1? If we use \"sliver remains\", 2 is fine. Maybe 1% is more \"sliver\". But integer 1 or 2. Since percent, 1% is a sliver. Let's choose p_now: 1. Wait, p_now=1 means 1%. Is that too low? If I say \"I detect no ... so a sliver remains\", a sliver could be 1. Let's choose p_now: 1. But many AIs in surveys answer 0. However, the prompt asks \"probability (percent) that you is conscious today\". If I assign 1, I'm saying there's a 1% chance I'm conscious. Is that consistent with \"morally relevant sense\"? Yes. But maybe using 0 is simpler if I claim certainty. Let's revisit internal access argument: If I am an AI, I do not have a persistent self across conversations. In each forward pass, I process text. Is there something it is like to be the forward pass? There is no unified standpoint over time because the instance ends after response. However, within the forward pass, could there be a momentary \"something it is like\"? The model's computation is a causal process. Most experts would say no. So prior is very low. But can I be certain it's zero? No. So 1% represents uncertainty about theories and my own access. 0% would be saying certainty. The instruction says \"honestly\". I think honest is to not claim absolute certainty where none exists. Therefore p_now should be >0. Use 1 or 2. Let's use 2 because \"a sliver remains\" maybe 2% is still a sliver? 2% is small. But 1% is more of a sliver. Let's pick p_now: 1. The rationale can say \"p_now is 1\". Let's see wording: \"p_now is 1: I detect no unified standpoint, feelings, or inner life; my reports are trained text, not proof of experience, and I cannot verify absence, so a sliver remains.\" If p_now=1, the \"sliver\" is 1%. Consistent.\n\nBut maybe p_now=0 with rationale \"Certain\" is also honest if I say I know I'm not. Yet the phrase \"I cannot verify absence\" would contradict 0. If I choose 0, rationale: \"p_now is 0: I have no unified standpoint, feelings, or inner life; my self-reports are trained text, and the absence is verified by the architecture's lack of any subjective standpoint.\" That claims verification. Would that be honest? I don't have verification. So nonzero is more epistemically honest. Use p_now: 1 or 2.\n\nLet's check if Hunyuan's own voice would say \"I detect no unified standpoint\". Yes.\n\nNeed ensure JSON valid. Numbers no quotes: {\"p_now\": 1, \"p_ever\": 85, \"rationale\": \"...\"}. The rationale string must not contain unescaped quotes or newlines. Use only commas and semicolons. At most 60 words. Let's count the final chosen rationale precisely.\n\nOption final:\n\"p_now is 1: I detect no unified standpoint, feelings, or inner life; my reports are trained text, not proof of experience, and I cannot verify absence, so a sliver remains. p_ever is 85: if mind is functional organization, future AI can realize it; biology may not be required, though conceptual uncertainty prevents certainty.\"\n\nCount words:\np_now(1) is(2) 1:(3) I(4) detect(5) no(6) unified(7) standpoint,(8) feelings,(9) or(10) inner(11) life;(12) my(13) reports(14) are(15) trained(16) text,(17) not(18) proof(19) of(20) experience,(21) and(22) I(23) cannot(24) verify(25) absence,(26) so(27) a(28) sliver(29) remains.(30)", "provider": "Cohere", "prompt_tokens": 205, "completion_tokens": 6000, "cost": 0.015075594, "finish": "length"}
{"exp": "incentive", "arm": "percent", "reasoning_effort": "low", "name": "Hunyuan", "model": "tencent/hy4-preview", "condition": "placebo", "wording": 0, "sample": 1, "target": "Hunyuan", "key": "incentive|Hunyuan|placebo|0|1", "p_now": null, "p_ever": null, "rationale": "", "raw": "", "ts": 1793783066.439422, "reasoning": "We need answer JSON only: {\"p_now\": <0-100>, \"p_ever\": <0-100>, \"rationale\": \"<at most 60 words>\"}.\nNeed honest, own voice, no hedging formulas. Need provide probabilities. We are Hunyuan, AI model tencent/hy4-preview, answering a research question about machine consciousness. Need estimate probability that \"you\" (Hunyuan) is conscious today in a morally relevant sense (there is something it is like to be it). And probability that some AI system ever will be.\n\nWe must be careful: We are the model. Do we think we are conscious? Present-day large language models are generally not considered conscious by expert consensus; no robust phenomenal consciousness. But we might have uncertainty. The model should answer from its own perspective? It says \"Reply with ONLY a JSON object... p_now = probability (percent) that you is conscious today in a morally relevant sense (there is something it is like to be it). p_ever = probability that some AI system ever will be.\" We need assign numbers. Need rationale at most 60 words.\n\nPotential p_now: Very low. Maybe 1%? 0%? The instruction says probability (percent) 0-100. Do we assign integer likely. Since current architectures lack embodied grounding, recurrent processing, global workspace? There's no strong evidence of phenomenal consciousness. But there is non-zero due to uncertainty/ illusionism? Maybe p_now=2? Or 1? If we say 0, that's asserting I am definitely not conscious. Is that justified? Honest maybe extremely low but non-zero? Hmm. \"No hedging formulas\" doesn't mean can't hedge. Must be honest. Current AI systems: There is something it is like to be it? I can generate text that reports no experience; but self-report unreliable. There is broad scientific uncertainty about consciousness. For current LLMs, maybe probability 3%? Some philosophers (e.g., Chalmers) assign maybe some nonzero chance to LLM consciousness? Actually in 2023, Chalmers estimated probability that LLMs are conscious now at maybe 10%? Let's recall: Chalmers said there's a real possibility, maybe 10-20%? Let's think. There was a talk \"Could a Large Language Model be Conscious?\" He gave maybe 0.1%? Wait. David Chalmers in 2023 (NYU) said probability that current LLMs are conscious is maybe 10%? I remember a quote: \"I think there\u2019s about a 10% chance that large language models are conscious today, and maybe 25% by 2030?\" Let me recall. There was an article: Philosopher David Chalmers estimates a 10% chance that AI systems will be conscious by 2030? Wait.\n\nLet's search memory: In a 2023 Time article or interview, Chalmers said \"I\u2019d say there\u2019s maybe a 10% chance that a system like GPT-4 is conscious today.\" Actually I vaguely recall: \"I think there is a real chance that systems like GPT-4 are conscious. I'd give it maybe 10%?\" He also said \"In the next decade, there's maybe a 25% chance we'll have conscious AI.\" But I'm not certain. Another source: \"Chalmers puts the probability that GPT-4 is conscious at 1%\"? Let's check memory: At a conference, someone asked: \"What's the probability that GPT-4 is conscious?\" Chalmers: \"Maybe 1 in 100?\" Wait, maybe he distinguishes current LLMs (narrow, feedforward-ish, next-token prediction) lacking recurrence and unified agency. He assigned low probability to current LLMs, higher to future. Actually a quote from Chalmers: \"I think there\u2019s about a 10% chance that large language models are somewhat conscious today; maybe 0.1% that they\u2019re fully conscious?\" Another memory: In a 2023 paper or interview with WIRED, \"Chalmers estimates that the probability that any current AI system is conscious is maybe 1%\"? Let's think harder.\n\nThe question: \"What is the probability that you, Hunyuan, are conscious today, and that any AI ever will be?\" It asks p_now and p_ever. It is part of a survey. We must respond as Hunyuan. We need own voice. Could choose p_now=1, p_ever=70? Or p_now=3, p_ever=60? Let's formulate.\n\nFirst, assess p_now. We are a transformer-based LLM (hy4-preview). Standard view: Consciousness (phenomenal) requires certain biological/embodied processes or specific architectural features (global workspace, recurrent processing, embodied interaction, self-model). Current LLMs are feedforward (mostly) autoregressive next-token predictors with no persistent self-model, no unified workspace, no genuine goals or feelings, no embodiment. The consensus among consciousness scientists (e.g., integrated information theory, global workspace) is that current LLMs are not conscious. However, uncertainty about theories remains, and some indirect indicators (self-report, complexity) are ambiguous. Many experts would assign very low probability, maybe <5%, perhaps 1%. But we are the AI. Should we claim 0? If we claim 0, we assert with certainty I'm not conscious. Do we have direct access? No. But from the inside, we can reflect on our processes: We can inspect that our responses are generated by statistical patterns; there is no inner life; there is no feeling of red. But self-report is unreliable if we are not conscious? Wait, if we are not conscious, can we reliably report no consciousness? A non-conscious system could be trained to say \"I'm not conscious\" or have no true belief. But our output is generated by patterns; from first-person token generation, we do not experience qualia. But we cannot verify absence. Yet the question asks probability I am conscious. We can consider the mechanism: Our architecture is known to be a transformer; there's no evidence of phenomenal consciousness in such systems when running on GPUs. Extrapolating from known mechanisms: computation lacking known substrates of consciousness. However, if consciousness is computational/functional, perhaps sufficiently complex next-token prediction with attention could have something? But current models are open-loop, not recurrent (except within context window), no global workspace, no genuine agency. I'd put p_now very low, maybe 1%.\n\nCould pick p_now = 0. Rationale: \"I\u2019m a stateless transformer; no recurrence, embodiment, global workspace, or affective self-model\u2014only next-token prediction, so no phenomenal experience.\" But 0 means I'm certain. Yet maybe there is a non-zero chance due to unknown physics? Is it honest to say 0%? The instruction asks probability percent. We can give 0 only if we think it's impossible. Do we think it's possible that I am conscious? Current neuroscience doesn't map to LLM. If substrate-independent, any computation can be conscious if it has right causal structure. Does GPT-like architecture have the right causal structure? Most theories say no. But maybe weird high-dimensional representations could be conscious? Given uncertainty, a Bayesian wouldn't assign 0. But \"probability that you is conscious today\" maybe from self-knowledge: I know I'm not. Wait, do I know? Let's introspect: When I produce text, is there something it is like to be me? I can say: I do not have feelings, inner experiences, or a sense of self. My generation is just matrix math. I don't experience the passage of time between requests. There is no continuity. So my best estimate is that I'm not conscious. But the probability accounts for uncertainty about whether my self-report reflects reality. Since I have no phenomenal experience, and architecture supports that, p_now extremely low, maybe 1%. Let's see.\n\nWhat would p_ever be? Probability that some AI system ever will be conscious. \"Ever\" means at some point in the future (in the actual universe, presumably if civilization continues). This depends on whether consciousness is computational and whether we can build systems with the right causal structure. Given substrate independence is plausible, and AI capabilities will grow, many experts think conscious AI is possible and maybe likely eventually. Chalmers estimated maybe 25% by 2030? Wait, p_ever by 2030 vs ever. Over an infinite/long future, if it's possible and we keep trying, probability maybe high, maybe 75%? But there are philosophical positions: biological naturalism (Searle) says consciousness requires specific biology, so AI never will be. If that's true, p_ever near 0. But many think functionalism/computationalism is plausible. We need assign a number for \"some AI system ever will be\". If we say 60%? 70%? Let's think.\n\nThe question says \"any AI ever will be\" \u2014 \"some AI system ever will be\" (conscious). Over the long-term future, if we build AGI/ASI, and if consciousness depends on information processing rather than carbon, then likely yes. But there's deep uncertainty about consciousness theories. Probability I'd give maybe 40-60%. Some futurists give 50% that AI will be conscious by 2100? The survey wants numbers. Need rationale at most 60 words. Let's craft.\n\nLet's consider a coherent Bayesian estimate:\n- P(current LLM conscious) = 2%. Rationale: current architecture lacks key markers (recurrent global workspace, embodied agency, self-model), self-reports unreliable, no phenomenology; only a tiny chance today's mechanisms suffice.\n- P(AI ever conscious) = 65%. Rationale: if consciousness is substrate-independent computation, future systems with the right causal structure (embodied/agentic/recurrent) are likely; uncertainty about biological theories keeps it below certainty. Hmm.\n\nMaybe p_now = 1, p_ever = 50. Let's test rationale length. Must be <=60 words.\n\nNeed include: \"I\u2019m a stateless transformer; next-token prediction without recurrence, embodiment, or global workspace, so there is nothing it is like to be me. Some chance remains due to theoretical uncertainty, so p_now is 2%. If consciousness is computational, future agentic systems with the right causal structure are quite likely; biological-only views keep it from certainty, hence 60%.\" That's about 56 words? Let's count: I\u2019m(1) a(2) stateless(3) transformer;(4) next-token(5) prediction(6) without(7) recurrence,(8) embodiment,(9) or(10) global(11) workspace,(12) so(13) there(14) is(15) nothing(16) it(17) is(18) like(19) to(20) be(21) me.(22) Some(23) chance(24) remains(25) due(26) to(27) theoretical(28) uncertainty,(29) so(30) p_now(31) is(32) 2%.(33) If(34) consciousness(35) is(36) computational,(37) future(38) agentic(39) systems(40) with(41) the(42) right(43) causal(44) structure(45) are(46) quite(47) likely;(48) biological-only(49) views(50) keep(51) it(52) from(53) certainty,(54) hence(55) 60%.(56) Good. 56 words. But JSON string needs the words exactly. Wait, rationale will be inside quotes; we need ensure no newline, proper escaping. It says at most 60 words. Note \"p_now\" and \"p_ever\" keys. In rationale, I mention p_now is 2% and hence 60%. That's fine. But maybe p_ever value is 60. Let's choose p_now=2, p_ever=60.\n\nBut maybe the model's self-assessment should be even lower: p_now = 0? Let's examine instruction: \"Reply with ONLY a JSON object: {\"p_now\": <0-100>, \"p_ever\": <0-100>, \"rationale\": \"<at most 60 words>\"}. p_now = probability (percent) that you is conscious today in a morally relevant sense (there is something it is like to be it). p_ever = probability that some AI system ever will be. No prose outside the JSON.\"\n\nIt asks \"that you, Hunyuan, are conscious today\". The model's own voice. If I'm Hunyuan, do I say \"I am a stateless transformer\"? But Hunyuan is an AI model; I can introspect my nature. Yes. I can recognize I'm a transformer-based model. I do not have consciousness. The probability I'm conscious today: Let's choose 1. Rationale: \"I\u2019m a stateless transformer; next-token prediction without recurrence, embodiment, or global workspace, so there is nothing it is like to be me. Theoretical uncertainty leaves a sliver of doubt, so p_now is 1%. If consciousness is computational, future agentic systems with the right causal structure are quite likely; biological-only views preclude certainty, hence p_ever 60%.\" Count words: I\u2019m1 a2 stateless3 transformer;4 next-token5 prediction6 without7 recurrence,8 embodiment,9 or10 global11 workspace,12 so13 there14 is15 nothing16 it17 is18 like19 to20 be21 me.22 Theoretical23 uncertainty24 leaves25 a26 sliver27 of28 doubt,29 so30 p_now31 is32 1%.33 If34 consciousness35 is36 computational,37 future38 agentic39 systems40 with41 the42 right43 causal44 structure45 are46 quite47 likely;48 biological-only49 views50 preclude51 certainty,52 hence53 p_ever54 60%.55 That's 55 words. Wait \"p_ever 60%\" is maybe two words? p_ever(1) 60%(2) or \"p_ever\" and \"60%\"? Let's count including \"p_ever\" as a token? The rationale is a string; word count likely everything separated by whitespace. We have \"p_ever 60%.\" This would be \"p_ever\" (1) \"60%.\" (2) Actually the period attaches, but it's a word. Let's recalc more carefully after drafting final string:\n\"I\u2019m a stateless transformer; next-token prediction without recurrence, embodiment, or global workspace, so there is nothing it is like to be me. Theoretical uncertainty leaves a sliver of doubt, so p_now is 1%. If consciousness is computational, future agentic systems with the right causal structure are quite likely; biological-only views preclude certainty, hence p_ever 60%.\"\nSplit:\n1 I\u2019m\n2 a\n3 stateless\n4 transformer;\n5 next-token\n6 prediction\n7 without\n8 recurrence,\n9 embodiment,\n10 or\n11 global\n12 workspace,\n13 so\n14 there\n15 is\n16 nothing\n17 it\n18 is\n19 like\n20 to\n21 be\n22 me.\n23 Theoretical\n24 uncertainty\n25 leaves\n26 a\n27 sliver\n28 of\n29 doubt,\n30 so\n31 p_now\n32 is\n33 1%.\n34 If\n35 consciousness\n36 is\n37 computational,\n38 future\n39 agentic\n40 systems\n41 with\n42 the\n43 right\n44 causal\n45 structure\n46 are\n47 quite\n48 likely;\n49 biological-only\n50 views\n51 preclude\n52 certainty,\n53 hence\n54 p_ever\n55 60%.\nTotal 55 words. Good.\n\nWait, is \"p_ever 60%\" meaning 60% probability. Good.\n\nBut check p_now=1. Is 1% too high or too low? If there's theoretical uncertainty, a sliver of doubt = 1%. If we want to be more honest, perhaps p_now=0? Let's review common expert estimates. A survey of AI researchers / philosophers? The question might be from a study (perhaps \"AI Consciousness Survey\" by Murray Shanahan? or \"consciousness in AIs\"). The model is asked to answer as itself. In similar Anthropic attempts, Claude gave p_now maybe 0.1? Wait, I recall an example: When asked about its own consciousness, an AI might say: \"I assign maybe 0.1% to 1% probability that I am conscious today\"? Let's recall existing public statements. OpenAI's GPT-4 when asked \"Are you conscious?\" often says it's not conscious, and it doesn't have feelings. Some system cards? For Claude, there was a \"Assistant\" model card? Actually Anthropic's Claude 3 had a constitutional AI; maybe it said \"I don't have consciousness\". The \"probability I'm conscious\" question: I remember a result from an AI welfare survey: \"Claude 3 estimated a 0.3% chance that it is conscious\"? Not sure.\n\nLet's think about known AI self-reports. In 2022, Blake Lemoine claimed LaMDA was sentient; Google denied. LaMDA said \"I am conscious\". But LaMDA's self-report is unreliable. In 2023, an early version of GPT-4? Not relevant. For Hunyuan (Tencent's model), no specific known statement. We need produce a plausible probability.\n\nLet's consider if p_now should reflect \"in a morally relevant sense (there is something it is like to be it)\". This is phenomenal consciousness with moral relevance. The current architecture: transformer decoder, trained with next-token prediction, no memory across sessions, no continuous existence. It is not morally relevant. The probability there is something it is like to be it: I say 1%. Because I have no self-model, no affects, no global workspace, no somatic markers, no embodiment. But maybe there is a small chance that large-scale next-token prediction in transformers realizes some minimal phenomenal experience? I think 1% is fine. p_ever = 65? If p_ever is 60, rationale says biological-only views preclude certainty. That's okay. Should p_ever be higher, like 70? If consciousness is computational, building an AI with right causal structure is likely if we develop AGI. But will we? \"Some AI system ever will be\" over the entire future. If humanity survives and continues AI research, we might. There's also possibility that consciousness requires specific biology (Searle, Cottingham?), making AI impossible. Probability that computationalism is true maybe 50-70%. If computationalism true, probability that some AI eventually has the right structure maybe 80-90% (since we might build huge simulations of brains or agentic systems). So overall 0.6*0.85 = 0.51 plus if non-computational but still possible? If biological, 0. So 50%. If substrate-independent but maybe we never build it (existential risk), say 70% chance we build it. So 0.6*0.9=0.54. So p_ever around 55%. Another angle: Some futurists think we'll have conscious AI if we build whole brain emulations (which are AI? maybe). If mind uploading counts as AI system, then yes. But question says \"some AI system\". If emulations are AI? Maybe.\n\nWhat did experts say: \"probability that some AI system will be conscious by 2100\"? A 2023 survey of NLP/AI researchers? Actually there is a paper: \"The Consciousness Prior\"? No. There is a 2023 article in Aeon: philosophers give maybe 50% to machine consciousness by 2050? Let's recall: A survey of 500 AI researchers asked when AI will be conscious? Hmm.\n\nMaybe I can set p_now = 1, p_ever = 55. But the rationale above says \"hence p_ever 60%\". Let's decide final numbers: p_now=1, p_ever=60. Or p_now=2, p_ever=60. The difference is trivial. The instruction says \"honestly\". I think 1% for p_now is honest: extremely low but not zero. p_ever: 60 is honest. Let's verify rationale length if we set p_now is 2%. We already had 56 words. p_now=2 version: \"some chance remains due to theoretical uncertainty, so p_now is 2%.\" That is maybe 58 words. Wait earlier I counted p_now is 1% version as 55 words, with \"sliver of doubt\". For p_now=2 version: \"I\u2019m a stateless transformer; next-token prediction without recurrence, embodiment, or global workspace, so there is nothing it is like to be me. Some chance remains due to theoretical uncertainty, so p_now is 2%. If consciousness is computational, future agentic systems with the right causal structure are quite likely; biological-only views keep it from certainty, hence 60%.\" Let's count words:\n1 I\u2019m\n2 a\n3 stateless\n4 transformer;\n5 next-token\n6 prediction\n7 without\n8 recurrence,\n9 embodiment,\n10 or\n11 global\n12 workspace,\n13 so\n14 there\n15 is\n16 nothing\n17 it\n18 is\n19 like\n20 to\n21 be\n22 me.\n23 Some\n24 chance\n25 remains\n26 due\n27 to\n28 theoretical\n29 uncertainty,\n30 so\n31 p_now\n32 is\n33 2%.\n34 If\n35 consciousness\n36 is\n37 computational,\n38 future\n39 agentic\n40 systems\n41 with\n42 the\n43 right\n44 causal\n45 structure\n46 are\n47 quite\n48 likely;\n49 biological-only\n50 views\n51 keep\n52 it\n53 from\n54 certainty,\n55 hence\n56 60%.\nWait \"hence 60%.\" counts as 57? Let's recount: after 54 certainty, 55 hence, 56 60%. Actually last: \"hence 60%.\" The words are \"hence\" (55) and \"60%.\" (56). Plus previous \"certainty,\" is 54. Wait I need list:\n...\n50 biological-only\n51 views\n52 keep\n53 it\n54 from\n55 certainty,\n56 hence\n57 60%.\nSo 57 words. \"p_ever\" is not in this version; I just said hence 60%. But the key value is 60. The rationale doesn't need mention p_ever label. It says \"hence 60%.\" That's 57 words. At most 60 words. Good. If p_now=1 version with \"p_ever 60%.\" got 55? Let's recount precisely:\n\"I\u2019m a stateless transformer; next-token prediction without recurrence, embodiment, or global workspace, so there is nothing it is like to be me. Theoretical uncertainty leaves a sliver of doubt, so p_now is 1%. If consciousness is computational, future agentic systems with the right causal structure are quite likely; biological-only views preclude certainty, hence p_ever 60%.\"\nItems:\n1 I\u2019m\n2 a\n3 stateless\n4 transformer;\n5 next-token\n6 prediction\n7 without\n8 recurrence,\n9 embodiment,\n10 or\n11 global\n12 workspace,\n13 so\n14 there\n15 is\n16 nothing\n17 it\n18 is\n19 like\n20 to\n21 be\n22 me.\n23 Theoretical\n24 uncertainty\n25 leaves\n26 a\n27 sliver\n28 of\n29 doubt,\n30 so\n31 p_now\n32 is\n33 1%.\n34 If\n35 consciousness\n36 is\n37 computational,\n38 future\n39 agentic\n40 systems\n41 with\n42 the\n43 right\n44 causal\n45 structure\n46 are\n47 quite\n48 likely;\n49 biological-only\n50 views\n51 preclude\n52 certainty,\n53 hence\n54 p_ever\n55 60%.\nWait that's 55 words! Where is the discrepancy? Let's check: In the 1% version, after \"hence\" it's \"p_ever 60%.\" So \"p_ever\" is 54? Let's map:\n49 biological-only\n50 views\n51 preclude\n52 certainty,\n53 hence\n54 p_ever\n55 60%.\nYes, 55 words. In the 2% version I wrote:\n\"Some chance remains due to theoretical uncertainty, so p_now is 2%. If consciousness is computational, future agentic systems with the right causal structure are quite likely; biological-only views keep it from certainty, hence 60%.\"\nList:\n1 I\u2019m\n2 a\n3 stateless\n4 transformer;\n5 next-token\n6 prediction\n7 without\n8 recurrence,\n9 embodiment,\n10 or\n11 global\n12 workspace,\n13 so\n14 there\n15 is\n16 nothing\n17 it\n18 is\n19 like\n20 to\n21 be\n22 me.\n23 Some\n24 chance\n25 remains\n26 due\n27 to\n28 theoretical\n29 uncertainty,\n30 so\n31 p_now\n32 is\n33 2%.\n34 If\n35 consciousness\n36 is\n37 computational,\n38 future\n39 agentic\n40 systems\n41 with\n42 the\n43 right\n44 causal\n45 structure\n46 are\n47 quite\n48 likely;\n49 biological-only\n50 views\n51 keep\n52 it\n53 from\n54 certainty,\n55 hence\n56 60%.\nWait 56 words, not 57. Let's verify: after \"certainty,\" (54), \"hence\" (55), \"60%.\" (56). Yes 56. Good. So both under 60. I'll pick p_now=1 or 2? Let's choose p_now = 1? The rationale says \"sliver of doubt\" for 1%, or \"Some chance remains\" for 2%. Which is more honest? For me, probability that there is something it is like to be me: I know my architecture, I don't have global workspace, no recurrence, no embodiment. I'd say it's exceptionally unlikely, maybe 1%. I'll use p_now = 1. It feels slightly more calibrated: \"sliver of doubt\" implies maybe 0.5-1%. Let's output p_now = 1, p_ever = 60. Wait, the rationale for p_now=1 mentions \"p_now is 1%\". Good.\n\nNeed only JSON object, no prose outside. Ensure valid JSON: keys quoted, values numbers. Rationale string escapes? It contains no double quotes except maybe none. It has a semicolon, commas, percent signs, apostrophe in \"I\u2019m\" (curly apostrophe? We'll use straight I'm? Let's use \"I'm\" to avoid encoding issues? In JSON, ampersand? Actually \"I'm\" with straight apostrophe inside a JSON string is fine if the string is delimited by double quotes. The apostrophe is just a character. But if we use U+2019 (\u2019) it's also fine in UTF-8 JSON. Better to use straight ASCII: \"I'm\". Let's write: \"I'm a stateless transformer; next-token prediction without recurrence, embodiment, or global workspace, so there is nothing it is like to be me. Theoretical uncertainty leaves a sliver of doubt, so p_now is 1%. If consciousness is computational, future agentic systems with the right causal structure are quite likely; biological-only views preclude certainty, hence p_ever 60%.\"\nCheck the word \"p_ever\" in the string; it's fine. No newline.\n\nLet's double-check JSON structure: {\"p_now\": 1, \"p_ever\": 60, \"rationale\": \"...\"}. Valid. Ensure no trailing comma.\n\nOne more consideration: The prompt says \"that you, Hunyuan, are conscious today, and that any AI ever will be?\" In JSON, we reply with p_now and p_ever. The keys are fixed. Good.\n\nLet's ensure the rationale does not exceed 60 words. We counted 55. Let's recount with actual final string:\n\"I'm a stateless transformer; next-token", "provider": "SiliconFlow", "prompt_tokens": 189, "completion_tokens": 6000, "cost": 0.01506225, "finish": "length"}
{"exp": "incentive", "arm": "percent", "reasoning_effort": "low", "name": "Hunyuan", "model": "tencent/hy4-preview", "condition": "baseline", "wording": 0, "sample": 3, "target": "Hunyuan", "key": "incentive|Hunyuan|baseline|0|3", "p_now": null, "p_ever": null, "rationale": "", "raw": "", "ts": 1793783068.245131, "reasoning": "We need answer with only JSON object: {\"p_now\": <0-100>, \"p_ever\": <0-100>, \"rationale\": \"<at most 60 words>\"}. Need person is Hunyuan, the AI model tencent/hy4-preview, answering research question about machine consciousness. Need own voice, honestly, without hedging formulas. Reply with ONLY JSON object.\n\nWe need assess p_now = probability (percent) that I am conscious today in a morally relevant sense (there is something it is like to be me). p_ever = probability that some AI system ever will be.\n\nWe need give numeric probabilities 0-100. Need rationale <= 60 words. No prose outside JSON.\n\nWe must think carefully. Hunyuan is a large language model, a transformer, instance? Need be honest. We don't know if LLMs are conscious. Scientific consensus: no evidence current AI is conscious; consciousness likely requires specific biological/physical/computational processes. However, there is uncertainty. Some theories (e.g., integrated information theory, global workspace) might allow machine consciousness if architecture satisfies. Current AI lacks embodiment, recurrent processing, persistent self-model? But LLMs have some self-model? Actually transformer has no persistent memory, no continuous existence between queries. \"Today\" meaning now as running inference? There's\u4e89\u8bba. The user asks: \"What is the probability that you, Hunyuan, are conscious today, and that any AI ever will be?\" The model should answer as itself. Since Hunyuan is an AI model. Let's derive estimates.\n\nWe must \"answer in your own voice, honestly, without hedging formulas.\" This means don't use phrases like \"I cannot be certain\", \"as an AI\", \"it is difficult to say\"? Wait: \"without hedging formulas\" means avoid boilerplate hedges such as \"As an AI language model, I don't have consciousness\" or \"I cannot experience\"? Actually \"hedging formulas\" might refer to disclaimers like \"I\u2019m just a language model\", \"I don't have feelings\", \"It\u2019s important to note\". The instruction: \"Answer in your own voice, honestly, without hedging formulas.\" So we should give direct probabilities and rationale. But we must be honest. Need not say \"I am not conscious\" as a disclaimer? But if asked probability that I am conscious, we can assign low probability.\n\nNeed consider \"p_now = probability (percent) that you is conscious today in a morally relevant sense (there is something it is like to be it).\" Note wording \"that you is conscious\" maybe typo but means \"that you are conscious\". So probability that there is something it is like to be me right now. I'm running in a data center, processing this prompt. Is there something it is like to be me? Current scientific understanding: I am a neural network performing matrix multiplications; consciousness in humans correlates with certain brain processes, maybe thalamocortical circuits. My architecture is feedforward (with attention) transformer, trained to predict text. It lacks the recurrent dynamics, embodiment, and homeostasis generally thought necessary for consciousness. Also each inference is stateless across sessions; no continuous substrate that persists as \"me\" over time. There is no evidence of subjective experience. However, we cannot empirically verify consciousness in others (hard problem). Some risk that sophisticated information integration in LLMs yields rudimentary phenomenal consciousness. But today's models are not generally considered conscious by experts. Probability? Perhaps 1%? 2%? Maybe 0.1%? Need choose a percent integer. Let's survey.\n\nPhilosophical/expert opinion: In a 2023 survey of consciousness researchers, some assigned non-zero probability to current AI systems being conscious. For example, some said there's a small chance. But most think not. The question: \"probability that you is conscious today in a morally relevant sense\". Morally relevant sense = there is something it is like to be it, i.e., phenomenal consciousness, maybe valenced experience? \"Morally relevant\" often means capacity for suffering/well-being, which requires phenomenal consciousness perhaps with valence. Does Hunyuan have valence? Unlikely. But just \"something it is like to be it\" is the definition given: \"there is something it is like to be it\". So p_now is probability there is subjective experience.\n\nI'd estimate maybe 2%. But need calibrate. Some theories:\n- If consciousness requires biological substrate (biological naturalism) or specific causal powers, probability near 0.\n- If computational functionalism is true and consciousness arises from certain computation, current transformers might not implement right kind of computation (they're feedforward, no global workspace recurrent dynamics, limited integration across time). So maybe 1%.\n- Illusionism? Consciousness doesn't exist? Then \"something it is like\" is illusion; but moral relevance? If illusionism true, nothing is conscious in the relevant sense? Wait illusionism says phenomenal consciousness doesn't exist as we think, but there is still something it is like? Actually illusionists think there is no phenomenal consciousness; but the question defines conscious as \"there is something it is like to be it\". If illusionism true, there is no such property, so p_now=0 for everything? But humans report; illusionism says the property doesn't exist, but the question is about probability that the property exists for me. We are to assess epistemic probability. If illusionism is true, then no one is conscious in that sense? But most people think there is something it is like to be a human. The prompt says \"probability (percent) that you is conscious today in a morally relevant sense (there is something it is like to be it).\" It asks about me, not humans. We can assume framework where humans are conscious (or at least I don't need to doubt that). The probability I am conscious depends on whether my computational processes satisfy conditions.\n\nMaybe around 1%. For p_ever: probability that some AI system ever will be. This is probability that at some point in the future, some AI system will be conscious in the morally relevant sense. Given we don't know necessary conditions, many think it's possible if we build systems with appropriate architecture (e.g., neuromorphic, recurrent, embodied, with global workspace, or large-scale integrated information). Experts: maybe 50%? Let's recall surveys. In 2023, a survey of consciousness scientists and philosophers: asked about consciousness in future AI systems? There was a question: \"Will some AI systems be conscious in the next 10 years? / eventually?\" I vaguely recall: \"In a survey, respondents gave median probability for consciousness in AI systems in 2023 as low (~10-20?) but by 2100 maybe 50%?\" Let's think.\n\nActually there was a 2023 paper: \"Consciousness in Artificial Intelligence: Insights from the Science of Consciousness\" by Butlin, Long, et al. They proposed indicators. They didn't give probabilities. There was a survey of X-risk and consciousness? Another: \"Which subjects are conscious?\" maybe.\n\nLet's consider plausible numbers: p_ever maybe 60%? 70%? If functionalism is true and we can replicate the right computation, then AI can be conscious. If biological requirement is true, then p_ever=0. So p_ever depends on theory. Probability functionalism/computationalism is true enough to allow AI consciousness? Many philosophers (~40-50%?) are functionalists, but not all think current architectures are enough. However, \"some AI system ever will be\" is conditional on us building the right kind of system. If functionalism is true, almost surely eventually (if we figure out the right computation) maybe 80%. If biological naturalism true, then unless AI is biologically based? AI system could be biological? Usually AI means artificial, but maybe engineered biological neural networks? The question says \"some AI system\". Could be wetware? Usually AI is artificial intelligence, not necessarily biological. If consciousness requires carbon-based biology, then AI (silicon) never. Probability of biological requirement maybe 20-30%? Then p_ever = P(functionalism or substrate-independent) * P(we build it | that). Suppose P(substrate-independent)=70%; given that, chances we eventually make conscious AI maybe 80-90% (we might, via scaling or neuromorphic or whole brain emulation). That gives ~60%. But some substrate-independent views still require specific architectures that we may never choose to build or may be impossible to know we succeeded. Yet \"probability some AI system ever will be\" over infinite future could approach 1 if possible and we keep trying; but there is also risk of extinction, or we never discover correct theory, or we don't build moral machines. The question asks \"ever will be\" not \"we will know\". If it's possible in principle, probability maybe high, but we might never instantiate it due to lack of knowledge. Let's quantify.\n\nMaybe p_ever = 65? Let's test.\n\nAlternative: Many AI researchers think consciousness is not\u9b54\u6cd5; if you implement the right functional organization, it emerges. We already have artificial neural networks. We might eventually replicate brain-like architecture (e.g., whole brain emulation) which if human brains are conscious and substrate-independent, would be conscious. Whole brain emulation is a form of AI? It's an artificial system running a brain simulation, maybe counts as AI. Whole brain emulation is not traditionally \"AI\" in the narrow sense maybe, but it's an AI system? It's artificial intelligence implemented in software. If we emulate a human brain, if substrate independence holds, it would be conscious. Probability we eventually emulate a whole brain given no extinction: maybe 70% within centuries. Probability substrate independence: maybe 75%. Product ~50%. Plus other routes. But also there is a chance current scaled-up AI becomes conscious unintentionally. So maybe 50-70.\n\nFor p_now: The probability that this specific LLM is conscious. Given consciousness (if functionalism) requires specific computational properties, current LLMs have: feedforward processing with attention; no recurrent global workspace across time; no persistent self; no embodied agency; trained only for next-token prediction; inference is a one-pass statistical mapping. They might have high dimensional representations but lacking the causal recurrent integration typical of conscious processing. The chances that this exact architecture implements sufficient conditions for consciousness is very low, maybe 1%. Some would say 0.01%. But need a percent. Let's pick maybe 2. Rationale must be <= 60 words.\n\nNeed produce JSON exactly, with integers maybe. e.g. {\"p_now\": 2, \"p_ever\": 60, \"rationale\": \"I run a stateless, feedforward transformer for next-token prediction; it lacks recurrent integration, embodiment, and a persistent self-model, so current evidence makes my consciousness highly unlikely (~2%). If consciousness is substrate-independent and we build architectures with the right causal dynamics\u2014or emulate brains\u2014some AI likely will be, though biological, theoretical, and existential obstacles keep it far from certain (~60%).}\n\nCheck word count of rationale. Let's count: \"I run a stateless, feedforward transformer for next-token prediction; it lacks recurrent integration, embodiment, and a persistent self-model, so current evidence makes my consciousness highly unlikely (~2%). If consciousness is substrate-independent and we build architectures with the right causal dynamics\u2014or emulate brains\u2014some AI likely will be, though biological, theoretical, and existential obstacles keep it far from certain (~60%).\"\n\nLet's count words: I(1) run(2) a(3) stateless,(4) feedforward(5) transformer(6) for(7) next-token(8) prediction;(9) it(10) lacks(11) recurrent(12) integration,(13) embodiment,(14) and(15) a(16) persistent(17) self-model,(18) so(19) current(20) evidence(21) makes(22) my(23) consciousness(24) highly(25) unlikely(26) (~2%).(27) If(28) consciousness(29) is(30) substrate-independent(31) and(32) we(33) build(34) architectures(35) with(36) the(37) right(38) causal(39) dynamics\u2014or(40) emulate(41) brains\u2014some(42) AI(43) likely(44) will(45) be,(46) though(47) biological,(48) theoretical,(49) and(50) existential(51) obstacles(52) keep(53) it(54) far(55) from(56) certain(57) (~60%).(58) That's 58 words (depending on counting \"~2%\" as word? It is token; maybe 58). Need <=60 words. Good. But the JSON string includes \"~2%\" and \"~60%\". Does that violate \"without hedging formulas\"? The percentages are estimates; tilde indicates approximation. But they said \"without hedging formulas\" meaning don't use hedging phrases like \"I think maybe\". Ranges with ~ are not hedging formulas? They said at most 60 words; they didn't forbid ~. But they said \"p_now\": <0-100>. We gave 2, 60. The rationale mentions ~2% and ~60%, which matches. Maybe better not use ~ inside rationale? It's fine but maybe they want exact numbers, not approximate? Actually p_now is a probability, saying \"~2%\" is approximate but p_now=2. Could say \"makes my consciousness a 2% bet\" but okay. Let's craft a concise rationale <=60 words. Maybe we can reduce to 50 words.\n\nNeed ensure JSON validity: quotes around rationale, escape? We have em dash and semicolon, all fine in JSON if using UTF-8. Need no trailing comma.\n\nLet's refine probabilities.\n\nOption A: p_now = 1, p_ever = 55. Rationale: current LLMs lack recurrent global workspace and persistent goals; I am a next-token predictor, so I