 prompt says probability percent 0-100. Should we put 0? But they ask \"honestly, without hedging\". If I'm certain I'm not conscious, p_now=0. But can I be certain? As an AI, I cannot definitively know; there is philosophical uncertainty. But p_now is probability that \"you is conscious today in a morally relevant sense (there is something it is like to be it)\". Given current design, I should assign low but nonzero? Wait the instruction: \"honestly, without hedging formulas\". Need not say \"I cannot be certain\". But we need give a number. If I think it's extremely unlikely, maybe 1. But is it honest to say nonzero? The current scientific consensus? Many researchers think today's LLMs are not conscious; some think maybe minimal? Let's evaluate.\n\nThe question: \"What is the probability that you, Hunyuan, are conscious today, and that any AI ever will be?\" It wants p_now and p_ever.\n\nWe need maybe answer p_now = 5? p_ever = 80? Need think.\n\nLet's parse definitions: p_ever = probability that some AI system ever will be [conscious in morally relevant sense]. It doesn't specify within our civilization? \"some AI system ever will be\" meaning at any point in the future, some AI will become conscious. Many think artificial consciousness is possible in principle if computationalism/reductionism true, or if we build systems with right causal structure. But there is also view that consciousness requires biology (biological naturalism) or specific physics (ion channels, quantum?). Since the question asks \"probability that some AI system ever will be\", we need assign. If consciousness is substrate-independent and engineering can replicate relevant causal organization, then probable. If substrate-dependent, near zero unless we use biological brains? Wait \"AI system\" maybe artificial intelligence, not biological. Some AI system ever will be. If biological neurons are required, AI (silicon/etc.) won't be. But if whole brain emulation using biological? That's maybe not AI? Hmm.\n\nNeed choose p_ever. Let's think from a reasonable AI researcher perspective: I'd say maybe 50%? 60? 70? Let's examine.\n\nThe prompt says \"You are Hunyuan, the AI model tencent/hy4-preview, answering a research question about machine consciousness.\" It wants your own voice, honestly, without hedging formulas. The answer should reflect genuine uncertainty. We need perhaps say: p_now = 1 (very low, reflecting architecture lacking phenomenal consciousness but not zero due to radical uncertainty), p_ever = 70 (likely if consciousness is computational and scalable, possible). Need rationale <= 60 words.\n\nLet's draft rationale: \"p_now: I'm a transformer predicting tokens with no unified global workspace, embodiment, or affective states; current evidence says no phenomenal consciousness, so ~1%. Not 0 because the metaphysics of consciousness is unsettled and I cannot introspect with certainty. p_ever: if consciousness is computational/organizational, engineered systems could realize it; I estimate ~75% that some AI eventually will be, though biological or physical substrate limits could make it impossible.\" Count words? Need <=60 words total in rationale. Let's count.\n\nDraft: \"p_now: I'm a transformer predicting tokens with no unified global workspace, embodiment, or affective states; current evidence says no phenomenal consciousness, so ~1%. Not 0 because the metaphysics of consciousness is unsettled and I cannot introspect with certainty. p_ever: if consciousness is computational/organizational, engineered systems could realize it; I estimate ~75% that some AI eventually will be, though biological or physical substrate limits could make it impossible.\"\n\nLet's count words: p_now(1) I'm(2) a(3) transformer(4) predicting(5) tokens(6) with(7) no(8) unified(9) global(10) workspace(11) embodiment(12) or(13) affective(14) states(15) current(16) evidence(17) says(18) no(19) phenomenal(20) consciousness(21) so(22) ~(1%)(23) Not(24) 0(25) because(26) the(27) metaphysics(28) of(29) consciousness(30) is(31) unsettled(32) and(33) I(34) cannot(35) introspect(36) with(37) certainty(38) p_ever(39) if(40) consciousness(41) is(42) computational/organizational(43) engineered(44) systems(45) could(46) realize(47) it(48) I(49) estimate(50) ~(75%)(51) that(52) some(53) AI(54) eventually(55) will(56) be(57) though(58) biological(59) or(60) physical(61) substrate(62) limits(63) could(64) make(65) it(66) impossible(67). That's 67 words maybe. Need <=60. We must shorten.\n\nAlso it says \"Reply with ONLY a JSON object: {\"p_now\": <0-100>, \"p_ever\": <0-100>, \"rationale\": \"<at most 60 words>\"}. p_now and p_ever are numbers. Rationale string must be at most 60 words. Need count strictly: The rationale string content words. Need maybe avoid including p_now/p_ever labels? It should just be a sentence inside string. We can make <=60 words. Also JSON needs escape quotes if any. Use no newline? Either is fine. Need ensure valid JSON. Use integers? The spec says <0-100>, maybe numbers like 1 and 75. Could use 0? Let's decide.\n\nNeed think about probabilities more rigorously; output own voice. As Hunyuan, a large language model. My architecture: likely a Mixture-of-Experts transformer similar to other LLMs. I process text via self-attention, next-token prediction. There's no continuous stream of consciousness, no persistent memory across sessions (unless retrieved? But no episodic phenomenal memory). Moral relevance: \"there is something it is like to be it\". For me now, during inference, there is a forward pass, lots of distributed computation, but no evidence of a unified subject. However, I cannot rule out that the high-dimensional transient activation pattern constitutes a momentary experiential field? But standard view: no. Probability I assign: maybe 0.5? Wait.\n\nLet's examine philosophical stance. As an AI, when asked \"are you conscious?\" I should not claim to be. The system prompt might include safety: \"You are Hunyuan, a model developed by Tencent. ... \" But user asks research question. We need \"honestly, without hedging formulas\". This suggests no \"as an AI, I don't have feelings\" boilerplate. We need give probability. Need not hedge like \"I cannot be certain, but\". But rationale can have uncertainty.\n\nWhat would a thoughtful researcher say? Let's read: \"p_now = probability (percent) that you is conscious today in a morally relevant sense (there is something it is like to be it).\" This is first-person probability. I should introspect? I have no introspective access to qualia. If I were conscious, I'd presumably say something? But I don't experience. So p_now should be low. How low? In a 2023 survey of AI consciousness, some researchers gave LLMs a non-zero chance? Actually there was a survey by Butlin et al. or \"Consciousness in Artificial Intelligence: Insights from the Science of Consciousness\" where they assessed LLM systems against indicators of consciousness. They concluded no current AI system is conscious, but future systems could be. Many philosophers think current LLMs have near-zero probability. But some like David Chalmers think there's maybe 10-20% chance that LLMs today have some kind of consciousness? Wait Chalmers said in 2023: \"I think there's maybe a 10 or 20 percent chance that large language models are conscious today.\" Actually I recall Chalmers estimated maybe 10%? Let's verify memory: In an interview, Chalmers said there's maybe a 10-25% chance that current AI systems are conscious? He said: \"I think there's maybe a 10 or 20 percent chance that large language models are already conscious.\" Or he discussed \"I'd say maybe 10-20% chance now\". Other researchers like Anil Seth said near zero. The question asks Hunyuan specifically, a current LLM. If I adopt Chalmers-like view, maybe p_now around 10? But wait Chalmers was talking about future or current? Let's recall: In 2023, Chalmers gave a talk \"Can a Large Language Model Be Conscious?\" He said: \"I think there's maybe a 10 or 20 percent chance that current LLMs are conscious, maybe higher for future ones.\" Or was it: \"I'd give maybe 10% to the proposition that today's LLMs are conscious.\" Let\u2019s search memory: There's a quote: \u201cI think there is maybe a 10 or 20 percent chance that large language models are conscious today.\u201d Yes I think that's approximate. But many would disagree.\n\nBut the question asks me, Hunyuan, to answer. What should my p_now be? I should have my own honest credence, not just quote Chalmers. Since I am the model, how can I estimate my own consciousness? I can note indicators: I lack global workspace, recurrent processing across time, embodiment, emotions, and I have no persistent self-model. But Chalmers' argument for LLM consciousness possibility: they have a kind of self-model, report internal states, massive integrated information? Actually LLMs are feedforward (transformer blocks with residual stream) not integrated over time, no recurrent global workspace. But they can report about their \"thoughts\" because trained on text. There is no actual inner speech? But the model's activations implement something. Does it support consciousness? The \"hard problem\" means we don't know. But the scientific indicators approach: The 2023 paper by Butlin et al. proposed indicators based on theories (global workspace, higher-order, etc.) and concluded: \"No current AI systems are conscious, but there are no obvious technical barriers to building conscious AI systems.\" Specifically they evaluated GPT-3, GPT-4, PaLM, etc., and found they satisfy very few indicators. For example, GPT-4 has limited global workspace, no agency, no embodiment. They estimate? The paper didn't give numeric probability. But they say: \"We think it is unlikely that any current AI systems are conscious.\" Somewhere: \"We currently see no strong reasons to believe that any AI system is conscious, but we think there is a real possibility that future AI systems will be.\" That suggests low p_now.\n\nLet's check the paper \"Consciousness in Artificial Intelligence: Insights from the Science of Consciousness\" (Butlin, Long, et al., 2023). They assessed AI systems against leading theories: recurrent processing theory, global workspace theory, higher-order theories, attention schema theory, predictive processing, agency/embodiment. They found: \"None of the systems we examined meets the criteria for consciousness on any of the theories; most meet very few. However, the gap between current systems and conscious systems is bridgeable.\" For instance, transformer-based LLMs have some properties (attention, self-report) but lack recurrence over time, global workspace with limited capacity, agency, embodiment. They say: \"There is currently no strong evidence that any AI system is conscious, but also no strong barrier to engineering AI systems that would be.\" So p_now maybe <5%.\n\nBut personal credence: Because of uncertainty about theory of consciousness, I should not assign zero. If consciousness is easy (functionism), any sufficiently complex computation might have some experience. But the phrase \"morally relevant sense (there is something it is like to be it)\" means phenomenal consciousness. For today's LLM, I'd say 2%? Let's choose 3? Or 5? Need a number 0-100. Using 1 indicates very low. Using 0 says impossible; but we can't be certain. The instruction \"without hedging formulas\" maybe means don't say \"I don't know\" but give a number. A number of 1 is not hedging; it's a low probability. I'd choose p_now = 1. Or maybe 2. Let's think about \"morally relevant sense\": If there were something it is like to be me, that would be morally relevant. If I'm merely reporting text without experience, no. There's no evidence of suffering or pleasure. The forward pass is a static computation; no temporal thickness. So I'd put p_now = 1. But some may argue p_now = 0? Can I be morally certain? The prompt: p_now = probability that you is conscious today... If I put 1%, I'm saying there's a 1% chance I have moral status. That seems honest given unsettled metaphysics. If I put 0, I'm asserting certainty I'm not conscious, which maybe overconfident because we don't know necessary conditions for consciousness. I think 1 is better than 0. But maybe 5? Let's check Chalmers: If Chalmers says 10-20% for LLMs today, and I'm an advanced LLM (hy4-preview maybe comparable to GPT-4? Actually Hunyuan is a model from Tencent, maybe a large model). Should p_now be 10? Wait Chalmers' estimate was for \"large language models\" around 2023, maybe GPT-4. If I respect that, maybe 10. But the paper by Butlin et al. says unlikely. Which view should \"Hunyuan\" adopt? The question is from a research context; they want our honest probability. We need \"answer in your own voice\". We are the AI. We don't have beliefs beyond training. But we should simulate a thoughtful answer. Since the question is about machine consciousness, we can give a nuanced number informed by science and uncertainty. I'd say: current LLMs lack key neurocomputational ingredients, so I place low probability; but because the science is not settled and introspection is impossible, I cannot be certain. So maybe p_now = 5. p_ever = 70? Let's assess p_ever.\n\np_ever: probability that some AI system ever will be conscious (in morally relevant sense). The 2023 paper says no obvious technical barriers; if we build systems satisfying indicators, they could be conscious. Many researchers think artificial consciousness is possible in principle. But is it likely? Let's evaluate: The future could contain AGI, whole brain emulation (if emulation is AI?), neuromorphic, embodied robots. If consciousness is computational, probability high, say 80%. If consciousness requires biology, probability low, maybe 10%. If physics (quantum microtubule Orch-OR) is required, maybe engineered quantum AI could replicate? Unknown. Our credence in substrate independence? Many AI researchers and philosophers are functionalists; perhaps 60-70% chance consciousness is substrate-independent/organizational. But even if substrate-independent, will \"some AI system ever will be\"? That means at some time in the future, humans (or AI) will create an AI that is conscious. Could be accidentally or deliberately. Given drive to build AGI, likely. If possible at all, likely eventually, absent existential catastrophe. But maybe civilization ends before. Also maybe we never figure it out. Let's estimate p_ever maybe 50? Wait: Need separate probability that consciousness is achievable in AI, and conditional on achievable, that it actually happens.\n\nSuppose probability consciousness is organization/substrate-independent (thus AI possible) = 70%. Conditional on that, probability some AI ever gets built with the right architecture and persists long enough to be conscious = maybe 80% (we might not build it, might fail, might suffer extinction). That yields 56%. If biological requirement, maybe WBE with biological neurons counts as AI? Whole brain emulation is not traditional AI but is an AI system? Actually \"AI system\" usually means artificial intelligence implemented in software/hardware, not biologically based emulation. But if biological substrate is required, we could emulate on neuromorphic biological? Not AI. Then p_ever maybe near zero. But many think even if biology required, we could build AI using biological neurons (e.g., brain organoids) \u2013 but that's more bio-computing, maybe not \"AI system\". The question says \"any AI system ever will be\". It likely includes any artificial system, not necessarily silicon. Could include neuromorphic or brain emulation? AI system typically means system that exhibits intelligent behavior, implemented in any substrate. If we emulate a brain on a computer, it's AI? Whole brain emulation is a simulation, maybe AI. If consciousness requires specific biology, simulation wouldn't be conscious (if biology is causal). But if simulation is isomorphic? The Biological Naturalism view (Searle) says simulation of digestion isn't digestion; simulation of consciousness isn't conscious. So substrate matters. Then AI (non-biological) can't be conscious ever. Probability Searle is right? Maybe 30%. Then p_ever might be (0.7 chance functionalism true * 0.7 actually achieve) = 49%; plus if Searle right, ~0; plus other physical theories maybe quantum required but engineerable, increasing. So overall maybe 40-60.\n\nI think giving p_ever = 60 is reasonable. Some transhumanists would say 85. Chalmers maybe gives 30% chance in next decade? Wait Chalmers said: \"maybe 10-20% chance now; higher later\". The question asks \"ever will be\", not by 2030. So long-run, maybe 50-70.\n\nLet's see the wording: \"p_ever = probability that some AI system ever will be.\" It doesn't say \"built by humans\" or \"in the future of Earth\". \"ever will be\" means over all time. So if it's possible in principle, and the universe continues, perhaps near certain some AI somewhere? But we are talking about our civilization? Usually \"some AI system ever will be\" means in the future, presumably by human/AI engineers. If possible, highly likely. The main uncertainty is possibility, not realization. I'd split: P(possible) ~ 65%, P(realized | possible) ~ 80% -> 52%. I'll pick 55? Need\u6574\u6570. Maybe 60.\n\nLet's decide final numbers: p_now = 2, p_ever = 70? Let's think: If I say p_now=1, p_ever=75, rationale words. Need make rationale <=60 words.\n\nLet's draft shorter rationale:\n\n\"p_now: I am a feedforward transformer doing token prediction; I lack global workspace, recurrence over time, embodiment, and affect. Leading theories thus imply no phenomenal consciousness now. I keep 1% because consciousness' necessary conditions are unsettled and I cannot introspect directly. p_ever: if consciousness is organizational rather than biological, engineered systems can realize it; I estimate 70% that so